WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:20.940
Tim Ferriss: Jake Muise. This is Tim Ferriss.
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:27.010
Show, where it is my job to interview friends,
foes, everyone in between, but the common

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:36.480
thread is world-class performance, and the
attempt is always to dig, to excavate, to

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:43.770
deconstruct the lessons, thought, frameworks,
and so on, that you can apply or test or simply

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:48.660
ponder in your own lives.
My guest today is a friend, Jake Muise. Jake

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:54.190
Muise is CEO at Maui Nui Venison, a company
he co-founded in 2017 that works to balance

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:59.440
invasive axis deer populations on the island
of Maui. Channeling that management into incredible

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.470
nutrient dense food. Maui Nui was selected
for Fast Company's "Top 10 Most Innovative

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:10.020
Companies in Agriculture of 2023," and its
venison has been served in top restaurants

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.000
across the country, including Alinea, which
featured very heavily in The 4-Hour Chef.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.890
It was a big section entirely because it's
so impressive. The French Laundry and Saison,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:25.310
where I just mentioned. I was one of the very
first investors when it was a popup with 12

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.030
seats, something like that. Josh Skenes, everybody
should check him out as well.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:33.979
Prior to Maui Nui, Jake was executive director
of the Axis Deer Institute for 12 years. Part

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:41.040
of a two-decades-long project focused on axis
deer in their long-term management in Hawai’i.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:49.180
You can find them at Maui Nui, I'll spell
that out for folks, M-A-U-I N-U-I venison.com,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:54.500
and you can find them on Instagram, Twitter,
et cetera, @MauiNuiVenison. I'm shocked those

00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:02.860
handles were available.
I think we will start where all good stories

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.600
start, and that's with lava.
Jake Muise: Oh, wow.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.630
Tim Ferriss: Fast-moving lava.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.950
Tim Ferriss: So I'm going to use that as the
cue and I'm going to let you run with it,

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.270
but let's begin with that.
Jake Muise: Thank you so much for having me.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:15.610
Tim Ferriss: Well, absolutely. It's good to
see you.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:26.220
Jake Muise: We're going to have a blast. Lava,
so in 2018, a fissure started erupting on

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:33.921
the East Rift Zone on the Big Island. It was
an area that was a lava zone previously, but

00:02:33.921 --> 00:02:39.170
had since been built over. So lots of homes.
There was this beautiful area called Kapoho,

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:45.830
which had amazing hot pools and tide pools
and, well, it's now gone, unfortunately.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:55.640
So anyway, fissure 8 starts erupting; it splits
into two, and it basically cuts off a several

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.620
thousand acre area homes, people, and this
lava flow is moving very quickly, 20 miles

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:06.450
an hour. So within a 24-hour period is about
three miles up the coast. It's moving slower

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:13.280
at the front, but essentially cuts off this
entire area. State and Feds arrive right away.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.440
They start helicoptering people and different
people out, people, plants, pets, whatever

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:24.510
people are holding dear.
Then a cattle rancher had been cut off, and

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:30.810
there were still 50-plus animals left between
these two flows. You can imagine it looked

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:38.830
like Mordor on the ground. Sulfur was terrible.
There was lava bonds. The fissure was constantly

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.810
spewing at about 100 to 200 feet.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, my God.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:46.610
Jake Muise: Yeah. So I think the math —
Tim Ferriss: It's not a simmer.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:55.049
Jake Muise: No, I think they said it was 26,000
cubic meters of lava per minute. It was absurd.

00:03:55.049 --> 00:04:00.810
It was like one of the fastest moving flows
they'd ever seen. So anyway, a rancher did

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:05.640
his best job to get the majority of his cows
out, but there are 50-plus animals trapped

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.060
between these two lava flows.
Tim Ferriss: That's when you saw the Bat Signal

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:12.770
in the sky.
Jake Muise: Well, kind of. We had happened

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:21.920
about a year ago to develop the first of its
kind live capture net system that with a helicopter,

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.410
you can essentially pick up cows live and
get them out of there. So if you can imagine

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:33.830
a cone-shaped net with a 20-foot diameter
frame on the bottom, and it's hanging from

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:39.820
a hundred foot line below a helicopter. So
a helicopter's moving, and this net is flowing

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:45.830
back and forth, and you place the net over
the cow, and then there's a switch at the

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:50.860
top of the cone and there's two lines.
Then, as the animal tries to move outside,

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:55.500
you let the first switch go at the top of
the cone and the net basically falls on top

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.610
of it. Then you just pick up the frame and
it's essentially sitting in a big bag evenly

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:05.370
distributed. Then you fly it out and you put
this bag down and you put the frame over it

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:10.740
and it just stands up and walks away.
Tim Ferriss: Got it. So the frame is basically

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.090
a rectangle or a square?
Jake Muise: It's an octagon. Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:15.330
Tim Ferriss: Octagon.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.581
Tim Ferriss: Okay, got it.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So federal, PETA, everybody

00:05:17.581 --> 00:05:21.910
gets ahold of us and says like, "We need to
get these cows out of here." And we were —

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:26.520
Tim Ferriss: That's because it was known,
at least to some subset of folks, that you

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.740
developed this live capture system.
Jake Muise: We were previously using it to

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:37.419
try and get cows out of high elevation critical
watershed areas where they were causing extensive

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:42.910
damage. So they get ahold of us. They run
us through what they want us to do. Of course,

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:47.120
I had to sit down on the team and say like,
"Do you want to try and rescue cows over a

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.550
20 mile per hour lava flow?" I think one of
the guys turned over and he is like, "We would

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:58.660
get to be a lava cowboy," and I was like,
"Oh, man. Come on, who's going to say no to

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:01.480
that one?" Ended up doing our due diligence.
It was pretty safe to do.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:07.060
But long story short. We got them all out.
But the first couple of days were so intense

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:13.500
and the very first cow, so we're driving out
there in the morning, it literally looks like

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:20.370
Mordor, the flow is creating its own weather
system. It's raining, it's dark. It's a two-mile

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:26.501
flow. So it's lighting up the entire area
in dark red, and you're driving towards it

00:06:26.501 --> 00:06:30.060
and you're thinking like, "Oh," and you're
passing all these national guards and you're

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:34.060
just like, "This doesn't seem a great idea,"
on your way out.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:37.590
We get all loaded up in the morning. The head
of PETA's there. It's this huge thing. There

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:44.590
had been so much loss already. They had lost
that entire community, hundreds of homes gone,

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:50.000
and there had been so much loss. The community
was starting to gather around this idea of,

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.660
"These things that were left behind, we could
actually get them." Get there in the morning.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:00.479
We get all set up, get dialed in with the
pilot. He had actually never done it before,

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:03.669
but it was the only helicopter big enough
that we could pick up those particular cows.

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:07.190
Name's Calvin Dorn. He's just an absolute
legend.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.310
So we fly out first thing in the morning heading
towards Fissure 8. It's fountaining, 200 feet

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:15.979
in the air. There's lava bombs that look like
they're going at the height of the helicopter.

00:07:15.979 --> 00:07:19.980
The cows are trapped right underneath it.
There's video. This we can put it in the show

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.260
notes.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, man.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:26.630
Jake Muise: — for people. It's crazy. He
goes to pick up the first cow, grabs the first

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:33.660
cow, does it all right. Then he goes to lift
and he's like, "Oh, this might be too heavy."

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:38.680
Tim Ferriss: Oh, no.
Jake Muise: I have my sulfur dioxide. I have

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:44.340
my monitors on. We're not supposed to get
on the ground. The net is attached to the

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:48.470
helicopter, and he is like, "What do we do?"
I was like, "Well, give it another tug. See

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.350
if you can get it off the ground." So he gives
it another tug and he gets it a little bit

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:55.680
off the ground, and I'm like, "Okay, can we
head downhill and you can get some elevation?"

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.069
He's like, "Here's what we're going to do.
We're going to use the heat from the lava

00:08:00.069 --> 00:08:04.319
flow to give us a boost."
Tim Ferriss: Ride the thermals.

00:08:04.319 --> 00:08:09.159
Jake Muise: "We're going to ride the thermals
up with this cow underneath us, and it should

00:08:09.159 --> 00:08:13.200
give us a big enough boost. We'll have enough
elevation and then we'll be able to get down

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.759
to the corral system."
Tim Ferriss: It's crazy enough that it just

00:08:15.759 --> 00:08:19.229
might work.
I'm thinking, I mean, you don't really have

00:08:19.229 --> 00:08:25.229
a choice. You're like, "Sure, let's try that."
So he gets this cow off the ground and he's

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:28.819
moving at a good speed. And we're picking
up a little elevation, and I can see the lava

00:08:28.819 --> 00:08:35.770
flow coming at 500 yards, 400 yards, 300 yards,
and we hit the flow and it's like somebody

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:39.959
kicked us in the nuts. We just went straight
up in the air. There was so much heat coming

00:08:39.959 --> 00:08:44.959
off the flow. I looked down to see what was
happening to the cow, because the cow was

00:08:44.959 --> 00:08:51.490
a hundred feet lower than us, and this cow
swings out, and then as it passes over the

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:56.510
top of the flow, all we see is just smoke.
Oh, no.

00:08:56.510 --> 00:09:00.850
Jake Muise: I thought like, "The head of PETA
is sitting down here."

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:02.991
Tim Ferriss: Watching my cow get vaporized.
Jake Muise: "I'm going to jail. Something

00:09:02.991 --> 00:09:07.090
terrible's going to happen to us. This thing
is over." We gain elevation and we start to

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:11.089
come out over the flow, and I'm looking down.
I'm like, "Oh, God." I had forgotten that

00:09:11.089 --> 00:09:18.400
it was pouring rain and it was soaked and
all of that water, it just instantly vaporized.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:24.950
It looked like the whole thing was essentially
on fire for a second. So we come out and I

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:31.339
see all of the water vapor clear, and I'm
like, "Oh, thank God." Come down, land, that

00:09:31.339 --> 00:09:36.370
animal gets out, walks away, happy as can
be. Then I think we've got seven or eight

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:40.260
more that day before they shut us down and
got a little crazy, and we ended up getting

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:46.410
all 50 animals out of that place. Happened
to be one of the most intense moments of my

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:51.700
life. Certainly, maybe not the most dangerous,
but I thought we were in a lot of trouble

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:57.300
for that one.
But it was just such a viscerally intense

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:03.310
scenario. You have these lava bombs going
off and these two flows, and but a really

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:09.470
cool experience in that community. There was
this celebration when everything for months

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:14.279
had been just — a lot of loss.
Tim Ferriss: So let's set the stage a bit

00:10:14.279 --> 00:10:22.010
by talking about the history of Hawai’i.
Maybe we could start for a second just with

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:26.660
the name Maui Nui. What does Maui Nui mean?
Because even people who live in the Lower

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:33.020
48 or continental US, many people have not
been to Hawai’i, and certainly people overseas,

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:36.260
many of them will not have been to Hawai’i.
But what does Maui Nui refer to?

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:43.060
Jake Muise: So Maui Nui refers to the three
islands of Maui, Molokaʻi, and Lānaʻi.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:47.269
And epochs ago, they used to be joined. There
used to be one giant island.

00:10:47.269 --> 00:10:51.260
Tim Ferriss: It's like the Pangea of Hawai’i.
Jake Muise: Yeah, and you can see it when

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:55.990
you look at topo maps or Google Earth, you
can see they used to be joined. So that's

00:10:55.990 --> 00:11:01.300
when we were deciding on a name. We didn't
know where management might take us, but axis

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:06.899
deer are located on those three islands. So
we landed at Maui Nui as kind of the name.

00:11:06.899 --> 00:11:11.620
Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Nui, is it fair to say, a suffix

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:15.350
that is used, or maybe it's just the order
of kind of noun and adjective, but big?

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.190
Jake Muise: Yes, sir. Good job.
Tim Ferriss: So you can say "Mahalo nui,"

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:19.530
like, "Thanks a lot," right?
Jake Muise: Yep.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:22.519
Tim Ferriss: Also for people, this may be
a way to get into the history also, because

00:11:22.519 --> 00:11:24.130
we were chatting a little bit about it.
Jake Muise: Yeah. Yeah.

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:29.160
Tim Ferriss: I've been spending more time
in New Zealand and I'm fascinated by Māori

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:36.070
culture, and there is a very close relationship
between what we're talking about and the culture

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:39.010
in New Zealand. Could you speak to that a
bit?

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:41.779
Jake Muise: Sure.
Tim Ferriss: Then after that, and you can

00:11:41.779 --> 00:11:45.370
tie this in however you'd like, but speak
to sort of the agriculture of pre-colonized

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.190
Hawai’i.
Jake Muise: Sure.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:51.490
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: So I'm going to speak to this

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:54.279
as best I can.
Tim Ferriss: Right. You're going to have to

00:11:54.279 --> 00:11:57.760
have. Actually let's hit pause for a quick
second, and because I think it's worth mentioning

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:01.589
up front. So a company co-founded in 2017,
who is your co-founder?

00:12:01.589 --> 00:12:04.870
Jake Muise: My extraordinary wife, Ku'ulani.
Tim Ferriss: She's amazing.

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:07.139
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Can you say a little bit about

00:12:07.139 --> 00:12:10.410
Ku'u for a second, Ku'ulani?
Jake Muise: Sure. I mean, first and foremost,

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:18.339
a mother of three amazing children. A brilliant
person all around. Funny, smart, beautiful.

00:12:18.339 --> 00:12:27.820
But she is a genealogist and a bit of a historian,
and she guides a lot of culture within what

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:35.110
we do. But she has such an in-depth understanding
of our place. She'd be somebody amazing to

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:39.709
have on if you ever wanted to really dig in.
But she's just this anchor for me personally

00:12:39.709 --> 00:12:43.790
and professionally to how we should be operating
in this place.

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.399
Tim Ferriss: How long has her family been
in Hawai’i?

00:12:46.399 --> 00:12:49.100
Jake Muise: Well, she'll tell you forever.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:52.770
Jake Muise: Exactly.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. She also, if I'm remembering

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:59.790
correctly, looks at old microfiche newspapers
and sort of excavates and translates.

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:07.230
Jake Muise: So post-Western contact when they
didn't have a written language prior to, but

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:14.740
post-Western contact, they became the most
literate society in a matter of 50 years.

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:22.199
I think 93 to 94 percent of the entire population
could read and write. In doing so, they created

00:13:22.199 --> 00:13:26.570
hundreds and hundreds of newspapers that she
shows me newspapers where there's a picture

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:30.830
of a drawn picture of a zebra, like, "Oh,
this thing is from Africa." It was extraordinary,

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:36.010
the level of information that they had. I
think they were also in understanding their

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:40.329
place post-Western contact and how quickly
their populations were declining. They were

00:13:40.329 --> 00:13:44.930
using it as this historical record.
So they were just writing everything down.

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:51.210
So what she helps to do is only two percent
of those newspapers have been translated.

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:56.980
98 percent of what could be their culture
is sitting in these newspapers ready to be

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:01.430
discovered, and it's just a treasure hunt
for her, every time she looks. She comes out

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:06.600
and it's just like her eyes are giant, and
she's like, "I found a new name for this rain."

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:12.120
She's going to write an amazing book one day.
Tim Ferriss: Also, I mean, she's very much

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:18.090
multi-hyphenate polymath because she's also
an incredible designer. Really incredibly

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:22.920
good and gifted. I hesitate to say gifted,
she is gifted, but that maybe sounds like

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:28.410
it minimizes the hard work and dedication
required to get good at it, and an incredible

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:36.240
writer also on top of that. So I wanted to
mention that because that underscores the

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:42.470
connection that she has and that you as a
family feel with the land. So I wanted to

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:51.430
just mention that up front and then we can
go back to the pre-colonial agriculture and

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:57.100
the tie between New Zealand.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So I think you could define

00:14:57.100 --> 00:15:04.380
this culture like literal geniuses. They were
able to navigate all of the Pacific Ocean

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:14.850
with extraordinary accuracy and directly speaking
about Aotearoa. So Aotearoa was settled from

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:18.280
Hawai’i.
Tim Ferriss: For people who don't know the

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:23.560
matching, so Aotearoa is the Māori word for
what is also known as New Zealand.

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:29.310
Jake Muise: New Zealand. Yeah. They kept such
amazing records of their voyages. My brother-in-law

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:34.199
got to spend a whole bunch of time there with
Māori in different communities. They have

00:15:34.199 --> 00:15:41.220
the actual boats, the canoes that were settled
from Hawai’i. They know the lineage of who

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:46.149
came on the boat, and they know where the
boats are. They have them hidden, and they

00:15:46.149 --> 00:15:51.889
know where the boats are, and that's how strongly
they can connect their ties back to Hawai’i.

00:15:51.889 --> 00:15:58.680
You just have to imagine that somebody that
could navigate the Pacific with that much

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:07.089
ease and accuracy, and it's extraordinary
to hear these stories. They're on the most

00:16:07.089 --> 00:16:12.840
isolated land mass on the planet with the
most finite resources, and so they developed,

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:19.161
as far as I know, some of the most ingenious
agricultural systems I've ever heard of. Great

00:16:19.161 --> 00:16:25.960
example, they're called the Kohala Field Systems.
They had 500 miles of what looked like from

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:34.920
the air swelled permaculture lines, and they
used 273 different varieties of sweet potato

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:40.959
to produce — oh, I just remember, they're
producing now yields 60 percent better than

00:16:40.959 --> 00:16:47.420
current sweet potato yields. You have to imagine
pre-Western contact, there were up to a million

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:49.860
people in the Hawaiian Islands.
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:54.860
Jake Muise: There's only 1.4 million people
now. So they had a million people on the most

00:16:54.860 --> 00:17:02.149
isolated land on the planet, and they were
feeding an extraordinary population with extremely

00:17:02.149 --> 00:17:09.309
finite resources and somehow were able to
maintain all of the biodiversity that I think

00:17:09.309 --> 00:17:13.350
they only know of a couple small species like
a flightless bird that was just too easy to

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:19.170
eat. They maintained extraordinary biodiversity,
which just really points to how connected

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:24.540
to that place where they had these amazing,
they call them Loko iʻa, but they were fish

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:26.920
ponds.
So I don't know if you remember when you were

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:32.140
there, but there's these huge rock walls that
come out into the ocean, near shore. Some

00:17:32.140 --> 00:17:38.400
of them are like 600 acres, and they had these
amazing aquaculture systems where they were

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:44.799
able to harvest fish constantly. They said
at peak performance, there were 500-plus fish

00:17:44.799 --> 00:17:52.690
ponds across the Hawaiian Islands producing
three million pounds of fish. That's like,

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:58.450
apart from what they were also catching outside
of those ponds as well. I think actually,

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:04.309
innovation is culture in that place, and she
continues to point me back to like, "I don't

00:18:04.309 --> 00:18:07.390
know, we're going to figure this out. We're
a part of this place. We're going to figure

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:12.300
out how to find balance in these places."
They just had some amazing, both agriculture

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:18.720
system, but also economics and social systems.
They had a particular system called an Ahupua'a

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:25.520
system, and it was mountain-to-ocean land
segregations that entire communities lived

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:33.130
in and managed, and they were managed as these
individual land segments. The entire system

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:39.350
was built from top to bottom to protect water.
So every feature of that system, how the food

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:45.840
was grown, how it entered the water, everything
was built to maintain good clean water.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:56.930
Tim Ferriss: So let's segue from that to axis
deer. What on Earth are axis deer, number

00:18:56.930 --> 00:19:06.789
one, what is the history of their presence
in Hawai’i? Why did they end up there? Then

00:19:06.789 --> 00:19:11.090
I have follow-up questions that relate to
water.

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:20.760
Jake Muise: So in 1868, then, Kamehameha IV
was given this gift and —

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:26.480
Tim Ferriss: Who was the leader at the time.
Jake Muise: Then-ruler of the Hawaiian Islands.

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:28.970
Tim Ferriss: The Hawaiian Islands.
Jake Muise: Came from India down the upper

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:34.880
Ganges to Hong Kong. She helped translate
the story, my wife did, Ku'u.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:38.040
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: Then they were moved from O'ahu

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:42.740
where they landed, and they were moved to
the small island of Molokaʻi, only 40 miles

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:48.289
long, 10 miles wide, and then a kapu, which
is a restriction, was placed on them for about

00:19:48.289 --> 00:19:50.290
15 to 20 years. And is —
Tim Ferriss: What does that mean?

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:53.080
Jake Muise: A kapu is nobody was allowed to
hunt them or touch them.

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:55.290
Tim Ferriss: And how many deer?
Jake Muise: Seven.

00:19:55.290 --> 00:20:02.040
Tim Ferriss: Seven.
Jake Muise: Yeah. As early as 1898, there's

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:07.230
literature that says they hired sharpshooters
from California — well, that was 1902. They

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:12.039
brought sharpshooters in from California because
there was already six to 7,000 of them. What's

00:20:12.039 --> 00:20:16.929
really unique about axis deer is they're one
of the only deer species in the world that

00:20:16.929 --> 00:20:22.100
can breed year-round. So most other deer species
will cast their antlers. Their antlers fall

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:26.909
off through the year. When that happens, their
testosterone levels drop significantly and

00:20:26.909 --> 00:20:30.610
their sperm is no longer valuable.
So axis deer are one of the only deer species

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:36.190
in the world that sperm stays viable year-round.
So it doesn't matter if a doe is missed in

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:44.270
oestrus. The current math that we have is
they are like 94 to 95 percent of them are

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:50.850
either lactating or pregnant year round. So
they just introduced an extraordinarily verile

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:57.280
species and have had profound impacts with
that type of growth rate.

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:05.350
Tim Ferriss: So if we then flash forward to
roughly 10 years ago or 12 years ago, what

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:10.390
did the situation look like in Hawai’i with
respect to axis deer, which have no natural

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:15.470
predators on that land mass?
Jake Muise: Yeah, on Molokaʻi.

00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:18.260
Tim Ferriss: In India, they certainly did.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So no natural predators,

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:25.150
like the capability to breed year-round, extraordinary
food sources, and being able to move up and

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:29.309
down elevation to find what they need. So
on the island of Molokaʻi where they were

00:21:29.309 --> 00:21:36.291
introduced, they're now at a — sustainable
capacity is the wrong word, but their population

00:21:36.291 --> 00:21:40.800
is only going up and down with available feed
every year. What's happening is they just

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:42.460
have massive die-offs, and it was actually
—

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:47.150
Tim Ferriss: What do the numbers look like?
Jake Muise: 70,000 and there's only 7,000

00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:48.529
people.
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:21:48.529 --> 00:21:53.049
Jake Muise: Yeah. It's actually one of the
reasons I got so interested in the subject

00:21:53.049 --> 00:21:58.740
as a whole is I think it was my second year
in college and introduced this amazing family,

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:06.320
and I was there hunting, trying to fill up
my freezer for college because I was broke.

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:13.289
I was there during a die-off. I remember coming
around this corner and seeing 40 to 50 animals

00:22:13.289 --> 00:22:18.600
just lying there, lethargic, essentially dying
of malnutrition, and it was an animal that

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:22.500
was introduced to me as something to love,
and it was precious. For the people of Molokaʻi,

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:28.250
it was this amazing food source. But that's
the only way they're currently balancing populations

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:33.090
is just this really sad thing that happens
every —

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:35.110
Tim Ferriss: Overpopulation famine, and then
it corrects.

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:38.920
Jake Muise: Yeah, and then it corrects. They
lose about a third of the population every

00:22:38.920 --> 00:22:44.190
seven to 10 years.
Tim Ferriss: What is the impact on the various

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:54.440
islands ecologically? Because I remember watching
first time, this video that you guys produced

00:22:54.440 --> 00:23:01.150
in collaboration with a number of other folks
and hearing voiceover, I think it was with

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:06.049
people talking about flying over certain areas
and saying, "Oh, wow, we didn't know the wildfire

00:23:06.049 --> 00:23:09.809
reached this far," and the answer was, "That's
not wildfire."

00:23:09.809 --> 00:23:17.340
Jake Muise: Yeah, because they can eat so
closely to the ground, and they're in such

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:23.029
large numbers, there can be herds of four
to 5,000. They're able to denude landscapes

00:23:23.029 --> 00:23:31.120
in days. The real issue with that is when
they do that in our watershed areas or high

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:38.900
elevation areas, those trees developed over
epochs thousands and thousands of years to

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:44.890
capture water. What ends up happening is when
we have some data from this, from a recent

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:51.370
study, those watersheds are operating at 50
percent of their previous capacity.

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:56.740
Tim Ferriss: So I guess there are multiple
issues at the consequences of that, right?

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:58.590
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: One is you're just not capturing

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:02.290
water, which is important if you're in the
middle of the ocean, unless you have a massive

00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:07.600
desalination program. You need the rainwater.
What is the effect now that has on runoff?

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:11.780
Jake Muise: Yeah. So when you think about
it from a — they call it Mauka to Makai,

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:18.250
like mountain-to-ocean impact. What all of
that denuded landscape is anytime we get any

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:23.490
type of significant rain, even a decent medium
rain these days, it's pulling all of that

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:31.380
topsoil, which took thousands of years to
make, and then it's depositing it on our reefs.

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:37.610
What ends up happening is they're smothering
both new and old coral and the coral die.

00:24:37.610 --> 00:24:43.130
So there's huge tracks of what used to be
some of the most pristine reefs in the world

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:47.920
that are now dead, and that also then impacts
nearshore fisheries.

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:52.890
So we just had this amazing conversation about
producing millions of pounds of fish pre-Western

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:59.909
contact, and now a lot of those reef systems
being very negatively impacted by that sediment

00:24:59.909 --> 00:25:08.820
deposit from runoff. Then when you look back
up slope, mid-range, call it 1,000 to 6,000

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:14.240
feet, is where our food systems in are. So
it's where cattle ranchers operates, and farmers

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:20.230
and coffee and all of these different, really
important — for as a function of food security,

00:25:20.230 --> 00:25:23.620
all these amazing foods that we're trying
to grow there. You can't grow them unless

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:27.909
you have a 10-foot fence at $45 a foot, and
even then, like —

00:25:27.909 --> 00:25:29.429
Tim Ferriss: $45 a foot.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:25:29.429 --> 00:25:33.070
Tim Ferriss: That's not cheap.
Jake Muise: No. So they are also having a

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:40.830
severe impact on our food system. So ecosystems,
food systems, nearshore, fisheries, and reefs,

00:25:40.830 --> 00:25:47.200
it is a compounding conversation.
Tim Ferriss: So to then take a look at how

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:56.539
seriously people in Hawai’i are taking this,
let's talk about the three-year hunt, and

00:25:56.539 --> 00:25:58.750
we'll build from there.
Jake Muise: Okay.

00:25:58.750 --> 00:26:03.270
Tim Ferriss: Actually, I'll just add one sidebar.
I'll put this in the show notes as well. For

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:08.679
people who are interested in learning more
about the incredible navigational skills that

00:26:08.679 --> 00:26:14.260
you're referring to, there's a really beautifully
written, detailed chapter in a book called

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:21.730
The Wayfinders by Wade Davis that talks about
the sort of Polynesian diaspora and their

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:26.620
ability to navigate, and it is mind-blowing.
It's a real accident. It's a real mistake,

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:30.529
rather, to think of some of these ancient
technologies as primitive.

00:26:30.529 --> 00:26:33.980
They're not always primitive. They're just
different sets of skills and technologies.

00:26:33.980 --> 00:26:40.279
But they would have the captain as one person,
the head of the ship, just making everything

00:26:40.279 --> 00:26:46.060
orchestrate. The navigator would be a separate
person, and they would basically sit like

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:50.740
a Zen monk and not sleep at all, because they're
tracking multiple currents. They're keeping

00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:56.130
track of where the boat is in space, even
when it's totally overcast, and they can't

00:26:56.130 --> 00:27:02.419
use the stars. I mean, it's unbelievable.
So I recommend people check out The Wayfinders

00:27:02.419 --> 00:27:04.850
by Wade Davis.
Three-year hunt.

00:27:04.850 --> 00:27:13.659
Jake Muise: Got it. So the Axis Institute,
which was what I started in college initially

00:27:13.659 --> 00:27:21.380
as a means to collect information because
nobody in India would answer me. Then became

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:29.250
very much a way to learn more and facilitate
some research. Four axis deer were illegally

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:33.200
introduced to the Big Island of Hawai’i.
So we've talked about Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi,

00:27:33.200 --> 00:27:37.150
and Maui. So four axis deer were illegally
introduced to the Big Island of Hawai’i,

00:27:37.150 --> 00:27:40.580
which was just about 10 years ago.
The Big Island, it's called the Big Island

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:46.799
for a reason. All of the Hawaiian Islands
can fit inside the Big Island, and it is also

00:27:46.799 --> 00:27:52.890
the food hub of Hawai’i. We grow all of
the vast majority of food on the Big Island,

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:59.280
and the impacts of axis here were already
really well known. So it was a state emergency,

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:02.760
when these animals were found, and the Axis
Deer Institute happened to be one of the few

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:08.039
people that knew a lot about axis deer. So
we were given that responsibility and contract

00:28:08.039 --> 00:28:14.919
to try and find and remove four deer from
a hundred square mile area. So they didn't

00:28:14.919 --> 00:28:17.740
know where they were. They —
Tim Ferriss: And these aren't elephants?

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:19.490
Jake Muise: Oh, no.
Tim Ferriss: What's the size profile? Just

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:25.300
so people can conjure an image.
Jake Muise: 150 pounds. Think about them standing

00:28:25.300 --> 00:28:28.669
three and a half, four feet high, maybe six
feet long.

00:28:28.669 --> 00:28:31.190
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: So a pretty small height.

00:28:31.190 --> 00:28:34.529
Tim Ferriss: So what are the biggest differences
for people who might be more familiar with

00:28:34.529 --> 00:28:36.980
say, whitetail?
Jake Muise: Well, the biggest difference is

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:44.279
where they evolved. Axis deer evolved with
Bengal tigers and leopards in dense jungle,

00:28:44.279 --> 00:28:51.700
and so they have this crazy sixth sense.
Tim Ferriss: Their agility is unbelievable,

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:58.950
and they're just vigilance, and sensory perception
is incredible to observe.

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:03.620
Jake Muise: I think because they didn't deal
with some of the evolutionary stresses of

00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:09.309
seasons in India, they've also developed this
amazing ability to adapt their home ranges

00:29:09.309 --> 00:29:14.169
to very safe areas. So on a typical home range,
could be a mile and a half to three miles

00:29:14.169 --> 00:29:19.130
in a given day, but when they find a safe
spot, they will stay in a very small area

00:29:19.130 --> 00:29:23.480
for an extended period of time.
Tim Ferriss: I see. So they move less than,

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:26.000
say, a whitetail, is that what you're saying?
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:29.810
Tim Ferriss: Therefore, they're better able
to denude a concentrated area.

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:33.779
Jake Muise: Yeah. So they'll find these little
cubby holes, these safe areas, and they'll

00:29:33.779 --> 00:29:37.539
completely denude these areas and they move
onto these next places. But for the project

00:29:37.539 --> 00:29:44.279
on the Big Island, which was literally a three-year
hunt, we worked every single day except Sunday

00:29:44.279 --> 00:29:48.019
for three years to find these four animals.
Tim Ferriss: Did they multiply in the meantime

00:29:48.019 --> 00:29:51.120
or no?
Jake Muise: Yeah, we ended up removing five,

00:29:51.120 --> 00:30:00.090
but it was this extraordinary exercise. It
took us seven months. Like you think you go

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:05.160
on an average hunt, it's three or four days
and you're already tired. It took us seven

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:12.450
months to get a camera trap image of the first
one. We had 50-plus camera traps all over.

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:15.150
Every piece of water we could find in this
a hundred square mile area, we hiked every

00:30:15.150 --> 00:30:17.090
day.
Tim Ferriss: Piece of water. You mean ponds

00:30:17.090 --> 00:30:19.480
or lakes?
Jake Muise: Ponds, blue rock, that water would

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:22.549
pool in, anything we could find, water troughs.
Tim Ferriss: Any water sources.

00:30:22.549 --> 00:30:27.860
Jake Muise: Yeah. So we finally get a camera
trap image. We're like, "Okay, we have idea

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:32.210
of [inaudible]." It takes us another four
months to get the first one.

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:35.350
Tim Ferriss: What do you do between? So you
set up the cameras, you're like, "We know

00:30:35.350 --> 00:30:36.630
these things. We know that all animals..."
well, not all of them. "We know these animals

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:43.470
need water," and set up the traps and takes
seven months. So you get an image.

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:45.600
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Boo, boo, boo, boo, boo. "Oh,

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:49.740
my God, we got our first image," and then
it takes another — how long?

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:52.529
Jake Muise: Three months.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. What are you doing in those

00:30:52.529 --> 00:30:56.320
three months?
Jake Muise: So two things are happening. One,

00:30:56.320 --> 00:31:03.450
we're doing ground transects looking for sign.
So you would love this. You're tracking every

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:07.100
single day. You're just looking for the tiniest
sign that they're in an area trying to —

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:10.059
Tim Ferriss: You used an expression “transect”
something or other —

00:31:10.059 --> 00:31:12.000
Jake Muise: Transects.
Tim Ferriss: So you're basically breaking

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:14.279
it into grids so you know what you've covered
and not covered?

00:31:14.279 --> 00:31:18.419
Jake Muise: Yeah. So you're gridding at — I
think we were doing 10 meters, massive areas.

00:31:18.419 --> 00:31:23.590
But what we also did in the process, which
we'll move into our later story, is we had

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:28.610
to find a way to increase detection rates.
So we started, and it was such a huge emergency

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:32.760
that the military was involved as well. So
we got to work with the military and utilize

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:38.450
some of their forward-looking infrared, both
helicopter mounted and binoculars. The minute

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:43.789
we started using that tool, we knew instantly
like, "This is the tool that's going to be

00:31:43.789 --> 00:31:46.659
able to find these animals."
Tim Ferriss: So forward-looking infrared,

00:31:46.659 --> 00:31:48.409
FLIR?
Jake Muise: FLIR. Yeah.

00:31:48.409 --> 00:31:53.940
Tim Ferriss: FLIR is, can we think of this
as sort of black and white Predator vision

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:55.519
from the Schwarzenegger movie?
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:31:55.519 --> 00:31:57.320
Tim Ferriss: Okay.
Jake Muise: Exactly. You put the mud on you.

00:31:57.320 --> 00:32:03.309
It's detecting heat and turning it into a
visual image for you to use. If you're cool

00:32:03.309 --> 00:32:07.529
or not emitting heat, can't find you.
Tim Ferriss: I posted a video you sent me

00:32:07.529 --> 00:32:14.440
of FLIR footage from a drone, which if you
want to see the scope, the magnitude of the

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:19.779
problem in terms of just the volume of animals
with respect to axis deer, you have to see

00:32:19.779 --> 00:32:20.779
this video.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:32:20.779 --> 00:32:24.160
Tim Ferriss: It'll blow your mind. I put it
on Instagram, we'll link to that in the show

00:32:24.160 --> 00:32:27.169
notes as well.
Jake Muise: Yeah. And we ran out of range.

00:32:27.169 --> 00:32:30.159
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Yeah, exactly. It's not
like they stopped.

00:32:30.159 --> 00:32:33.519
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: But it looks like a veritable

00:32:33.519 --> 00:32:34.769
New York City of axis deer.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:32:34.769 --> 00:32:35.809
Tim Ferriss: At night.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:32:35.809 --> 00:32:39.010
Tim Ferriss: Unbelievable.
Jake Muise: So this particular technology,

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:43.870
which significantly increases detection rates
both from the ground but from a helicopter

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:47.649
platform as well, especially when looking
straight down, you don't have the vertical

00:32:47.649 --> 00:32:51.340
layering of vegetation, you're able to look
straight down through vegetation and pick

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:56.980
up different heat signatures, that's actually
how we ended up finding them after the camera

00:32:56.980 --> 00:33:02.019
trap image is we started flying on a constant
basis and then we figured out where they were,

00:33:02.019 --> 00:33:05.929
took a week to put a plan together, and then
we were able to remove —

00:33:05.929 --> 00:33:08.960
Tim Ferriss: When was this? What year was
this?

00:33:08.960 --> 00:33:11.179
Jake Muise: 11 years ago.
Tim Ferriss: 11 years ago.

00:33:11.179 --> 00:33:12.669
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So were you using quadcopters

00:33:12.669 --> 00:33:16.630
or a different type of drone?
Jake Muise: No, we were flying out of a helicopter.

00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:18.850
Tim Ferriss: A helicopter. I was going to
say, this would have been very early days

00:33:18.850 --> 00:33:19.850
—
Jake Muise: Oh, yeah.

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:22.549
Tim Ferriss: Of any type of drone technology.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So we were out of a helicopter,

00:33:22.549 --> 00:33:28.520
leaning out of the helicopter with a binocular.
Yeah, it was not a lot of fun. And we were

00:33:28.520 --> 00:33:32.320
doing it four or five hours a day.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. That won't make you motion

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:35.340
sick or anything.
Jake Muise: Yeah. Yeah, well I've got lots

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:42.340
of those stories. Anyway, takes us another
two years essentially, but utilizing that

00:33:42.340 --> 00:33:47.909
technology, we're able to find and remove
all of those deer including the two that had

00:33:47.909 --> 00:33:54.510
been born. And there were very few invasive
species projects that are successful in Hawai’i,

00:33:54.510 --> 00:33:59.049
period.
Really cool fact, introduction of species

00:33:59.049 --> 00:34:07.630
to Hawai’i pre-human contact was every 25
to 50,000 years. It was whatever came through

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:09.190
wind or water, right?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:34:09.190 --> 00:34:14.070
Jake Muise: There is a new species being introduced
every eight days at this point.

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:19.810
Tim Ferriss: God, yeah.
Jake Muise: And again, the perfect place to

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:21.510
grow anything.
Tim Ferriss: So side note, and this might

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:27.730
be a total useless side alley that I'm taking
us down, why are there so many chickens in

00:34:27.730 --> 00:34:30.790
—
Jake Muise: Oh, I love this story. Oh, okay,

00:34:30.790 --> 00:34:32.830
here we go.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:34:32.830 --> 00:34:40.649
Jake Muise: They bring in mongoose, okay?
The powers that be decided we're going to

00:34:40.649 --> 00:34:46.099
get rid of the rat population by bringing
in mongoose, one of their predators. Well

00:34:46.099 --> 00:34:52.910
they didn't realize that mongoose are awake
during the day and rats are awake at night.

00:34:52.910 --> 00:34:55.790
Tim Ferriss: Oops.
Jake Muise: Oops. Giant oops.

00:34:55.790 --> 00:34:59.190
Tim Ferriss: And guess what? Mongoose are
really good at killing other things.

00:34:59.190 --> 00:35:03.599
Jake Muise: So including our native birds,
which the introduction of some of these species

00:35:03.599 --> 00:35:11.550
are so absurd. Anyway, so on the Big Island,
Maui, Moloka'i, I think — yeah, they have

00:35:11.550 --> 00:35:15.420
mongoose and there's no chickens. Guess which
island doesn't have mongoose? Kaua'i.

00:35:15.420 --> 00:35:20.530
Tim Ferriss: Why don't they have mongoose?
Jake Muise: They came to the dock, they were

00:35:20.530 --> 00:35:24.650
introducing them to each island. They came
to the dock and somebody at the dock was smart

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:30.690
enough and he booted them off and drowned
them. He booted them off the dock and drowned

00:35:30.690 --> 00:35:31.690
them.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:35:31.690 --> 00:35:36.060
Jake Muise: And it's just this amazing contrast
of you can't find a chicken on another island

00:35:36.060 --> 00:35:40.950
on the side of the road and you can drive
on Kaua'i and see a thousand chickens in 30

00:35:40.950 --> 00:35:46.340
minutes on the side of the road.
Tim Ferriss: So wild. I mean these highly

00:35:46.340 --> 00:35:52.990
biodiverse isolated environments are so beautiful
and compelling and also so fragile to disruption.

00:35:52.990 --> 00:35:55.130
True with New Zealand as well.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:35:55.130 --> 00:35:58.760
Tim Ferriss: I mean in terms of biosecurity,
it is one of the highest priorities.

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:04.090
Jake Muise: Right. And they do an amazing
job. Hawai’i is doing better, but they've

00:36:04.090 --> 00:36:11.201
done a very poor job over the years in restricting
what's coming into the island. So yeah, back

00:36:11.201 --> 00:36:16.430
to that three-year hunt. So successful in
removing an invasive species that there could

00:36:16.430 --> 00:36:21.010
have been a million-plus axis deer on the
Big Island. That's how large the Big Island

00:36:21.010 --> 00:36:27.990
is. And would've been absolutely devastating
as we talked about before, food systems, watersheds,

00:36:27.990 --> 00:36:30.140
nearshore fisheries, et cetera, et cetera.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:35.579
Jake Muise: And then that was actually the
jumping off point for what is now Maui Nui

00:36:35.579 --> 00:36:43.380
is some ranchers on Maui said, "Well, wait
a second, you guys actually did that?" And

00:36:43.380 --> 00:36:47.760
you have this technology so what we also developed
with that third technology is highly accurate

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:52.470
surveys. So within 95 percent, we can tell
you exactly the number of deer that are there.

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:56.290
Tim Ferriss: And how do you do that?
Jake Muise: So transects again at 400 meters.

00:36:56.290 --> 00:36:58.280
Tim Ferriss: And then you take a sample.
Jake Muise: No.

00:36:58.280 --> 00:37:01.510
Tim Ferriss: You don't do a sample count,
because a lot of people do that, right, and

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:03.450
then they multiply it out.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So they do something called

00:37:03.450 --> 00:37:08.250
distance sampling, which is they fly a certain
number of transects and then use math to basically

00:37:08.250 --> 00:37:14.310
extend what they've found and make a guess
over a period. And so we do a complete transect

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:19.570
of the entire area at 400 meters with a 20
percent overlap so we can have a confidence

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:25.570
interval of the number of times we've detected
deer twice. And we have done several projects

00:37:25.570 --> 00:37:31.750
where it's in a large, fenced area of a couple
thousand acres and we survey, we remove deer,

00:37:31.750 --> 00:37:34.760
we survey again, and we're never off by more
than five percent.

00:37:34.760 --> 00:37:38.530
Tim Ferriss: That's wild.
Jake Muise: So we have a tool that can accurately

00:37:38.530 --> 00:37:43.200
tell us the exact number of deer and the impact
of our management. And it's a tool, it's just

00:37:43.200 --> 00:37:48.470
super important because otherwise I think
what we do would be irresponsible without

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:51.800
that level of data.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, without that level of precision.

00:37:51.800 --> 00:37:56.200
Jake Muise: The folks on Maui said, "If you
can do that, come over here." And they were

00:37:56.200 --> 00:38:01.480
still calling them spotted rats. That was
their relationship. They had been introduced

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:07.570
to Maui in 1959, in 1960, so they weren't
as culturally ingrained as they are on Moloka'i.

00:38:07.570 --> 00:38:12.220
Tim Ferriss: And just to paint a picture for
folks, so the spotted, the axis deer, please

00:38:12.220 --> 00:38:17.119
fact check the shit out of this if I get anything
wrong, but they're very streamlined animals.

00:38:17.119 --> 00:38:25.970
They're not as beefy as whitetail. Their antlers
are angled more backwards. And they have,

00:38:25.970 --> 00:38:32.800
I'd say just generally speaking, very tawny,
burnt orange color with white spots.

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:34.850
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: And that's hence the spotted

00:38:34.850 --> 00:38:36.619
rats.
Jake Muise: Yeah. And that dappled coloring

00:38:36.619 --> 00:38:44.050
was used in those jungle environments and
they camouflage between there and grassland,

00:38:44.050 --> 00:38:48.109
it's amazing how well they'll move.
Tim Ferriss: Japanese deer are the same, it's

00:38:48.109 --> 00:38:52.849
a very similar pattern. Yeah.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So it started the, "Oh,

00:38:52.849 --> 00:38:58.060
wait a second, if you can be successful with
a project at that scale, what can you do over

00:38:58.060 --> 00:39:08.171
here on Maui?" And that was actually the first
phone call to the USDA to say, "How do we

00:39:08.171 --> 00:39:13.349
use these animals?" Because they were talking
about having us remove thousands and thousands

00:39:13.349 --> 00:39:17.390
of animals and they didn't care what we did
with them. They knew there wasn't a solution.

00:39:17.390 --> 00:39:21.790
We couldn't use them for food and we couldn't
donate them, they still needed to come under

00:39:21.790 --> 00:39:26.400
some type of food safety inspection program
in order to donate them to food banks and

00:39:26.400 --> 00:39:31.490
stuff. So that was the first call to the USDA
to say, "Well, wait a second, what would be

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:34.100
the process?" Because there were no rules
in place.

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:35.100
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: What would be the process to —

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:36.960
Tim Ferriss: Well, it was just considered
off the table, right?

00:39:36.960 --> 00:39:38.950
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So there are rules that just

00:39:38.950 --> 00:39:43.960
never been applied to this type of harvesting.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So that three-year hunt

00:39:43.960 --> 00:39:50.109
was the springboard into successful project
completion in that place where there's still

00:39:50.109 --> 00:39:55.650
no deer and potentially had a massive impact.
And then the springboard to Maui to try and

00:39:55.650 --> 00:39:58.670
do something more.
Tim Ferriss: So I'm going to do a little flashback,

00:39:58.670 --> 00:40:06.369
Austin Powers-style or Wayne's World, maybe,
get my Mike Meyers stuff mixed up. Did you

00:40:06.369 --> 00:40:12.700
learn about confidence intervals playing volleyball?
Jake Muise: I think actually no, it was in

00:40:12.700 --> 00:40:18.141
the worst class of my life, which was business
statistics in college. But —

00:40:18.141 --> 00:40:19.460
Tim Ferriss: Ended up being useful.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:40:19.460 --> 00:40:22.020
Tim Ferriss: You can't imagine how much of
that stuff actually comes back.

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:24.849
Jake Muise: Yeah. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: All right. So of course I'm teasing

00:40:24.849 --> 00:40:30.040
a bit by bringing up the volleyball. Just
briefly so people have a little bit more color.

00:40:30.040 --> 00:40:31.960
Jake Muise: Sure.
Tim Ferriss: Where does volleyball fit into

00:40:31.960 --> 00:40:34.190
your life and where does free pizza fit into
your life?

00:40:34.190 --> 00:40:39.190
Jake Muise: Okay. Was a classic Canadian kid,
played a lot of hockey until we were too broke

00:40:39.190 --> 00:40:40.851
to play hockey anymore.
Tim Ferriss: Wait, how do you get too — okay,

00:40:40.851 --> 00:40:43.640
too broke to play hockey, just too much gear?
Jake Muise: Yeah, too much gear, growing too

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:45.369
fast.
Tim Ferriss: Ah, right. I forgot about that.

00:40:45.369 --> 00:40:47.280
Right, it would be replacing your ski equipment
every year or two.

00:40:47.280 --> 00:40:49.640
Jake Muise: I remember my dad sitting down
and being like, "I know you love it, but you

00:40:49.640 --> 00:40:51.180
need to pick a different sport."
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:54.830
Jake Muise: And they're like, "Volleyball
requires knee pads, that's all you need."

00:40:54.830 --> 00:40:56.790
Tim Ferriss: Your knees shouldn't grow as
much.

00:40:56.790 --> 00:40:58.890
Jake Muise: "Here's a great sport for you
to try."

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:01.210
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: Ended up falling in love with

00:41:01.210 --> 00:41:06.819
it, just an extraordinary sport, redirecting
balls, moving a hundred-plus miles an hour.

00:41:06.819 --> 00:41:11.580
Luckily, got pretty good at it. Spent a lot
of time with our Canadian national teams in

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:17.119
different forms and then was recruited to
play at the University of Hawai’i. I was

00:41:17.119 --> 00:41:20.829
a decent enough player. I actually think it
was — I sent them a video of surfing. We

00:41:20.829 --> 00:41:24.540
started surfing on the east coast of Canada
when we moved out there. And there's six feet

00:41:24.540 --> 00:41:27.849
of snow and a tiny little ice floating around
and they're like, "Well if this kid can do

00:41:27.849 --> 00:41:35.530
this, he'll probably turn into a good player."
Played four or five years there where I became

00:41:35.530 --> 00:41:41.430
a family member for that family on Moloka'i
where, we talked about earlier, was able to

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:44.920
travel because the east coast of Canada was
so far. Really lucky to play professional

00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:51.200
volleyball for three years. Got to play throughout
Europe, the Maldives, Indonesia.

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:56.540
Tim Ferriss: So, pause, can you frame for
folks who don't have the context, a lot of

00:41:56.540 --> 00:42:02.360
people, how popular is volleyball? It's not
something that people in the US generally

00:42:02.360 --> 00:42:05.780
watch at all.
Jake Muise: Yeah. In Canada, there was nobody.

00:42:05.780 --> 00:42:08.290
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: It was my mom and dad cheering

00:42:08.290 --> 00:42:09.290
us on.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:42:09.290 --> 00:42:13.990
Jake Muise: But Hawai’i happened to be — it's
the mecca of volleyball. They have a —

00:42:13.990 --> 00:42:15.730
Tim Ferriss: Wait, worldwide or just in the
US?

00:42:15.730 --> 00:42:20.660
Jake Muise: Oh, just in the US. They actually
won the last two national championships over

00:42:20.660 --> 00:42:27.720
the last couple years, but they have a 20,000-seat
stadium that they filled on a regular basis

00:42:27.720 --> 00:42:31.490
for men's volleyball.
Tim Ferriss: And then you go overseas to Europe.

00:42:31.490 --> 00:42:34.880
Do people watch volleyball in Europe?
Jake Muise: Yeah, it's huge. So volleyball

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:40.720
is the second sport to football or soccer.
So you were associated with another club.

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:43.490
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: And you just got basically spillover

00:42:43.490 --> 00:42:49.720
of all the soccer fans or football fans. But
a huge professional league there that for

00:42:49.720 --> 00:42:55.109
great players pays extraordinarily well. And
for me, which was not a great player, was

00:42:55.109 --> 00:43:00.330
this amazing opportunity to go to Europe,
see all of these different places. And the

00:43:00.330 --> 00:43:07.380
professional teams only ever accepted two
international players. So you were embedded

00:43:07.380 --> 00:43:11.850
and immediately assimilated into whatever
culture you were in, which it's just such

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:16.150
an amazing way to be a part of those places
versus some outsider, right? So that leads

00:43:16.150 --> 00:43:22.700
into the story of free pizza. So my first
week playing in Harlingen, which is in northern

00:43:22.700 --> 00:43:23.770
Holland.
Tim Ferriss: Gesundheit!

00:43:23.770 --> 00:43:29.010
Jake Muise: Yeah, yeah. Yeah, well that plays
into this story. I had a week of practice

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:35.380
and our first game was coming up and I was
still very much getting used to the language.

00:43:35.380 --> 00:43:43.030
The coach was this 6'8 Olympic legend. He
was this legendary Dutch player. And I think

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:46.760
he was happy I was there, but I was just another
player. And you're paid to be there, so you

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:48.470
play really well.
Tim Ferriss: He's trying to mime with you

00:43:48.470 --> 00:43:51.390
to get things done, right?
Jake Muise: He's not giving it a lot of effort.

00:43:51.390 --> 00:43:55.080
And then we're playing one of the best teams
in the league for the very first game, we're

00:43:55.080 --> 00:44:02.070
doing really well. He puts a play together
to finish the game. I had no idea what he

00:44:02.070 --> 00:44:05.520
was saying, okay?
Tim Ferriss: What could go wrong?

00:44:05.520 --> 00:44:12.280
Jake Muise: Completely screw it up, embarrassing
level screw up, and we lose. And after the

00:44:12.280 --> 00:44:16.940
game, he comes aside in broken English and
he's just like, "You play better or we fire

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:21.290
you." You weren't playing for fun anymore
in college, you were getting paid to play

00:44:21.290 --> 00:44:27.240
really well. And the reason I didn't play
well is because I couldn't understand what

00:44:27.240 --> 00:44:28.240
he was saying.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:44:28.240 --> 00:44:34.730
Jake Muise: So the very next day we were reviewing
film and he was going off again and I didn't

00:44:34.730 --> 00:44:38.680
understand what it was. And I just remember
putting my hand up and saying, "Does that

00:44:38.680 --> 00:44:43.859
mean we get free pizza after the game?" And
he was like, "What?" And I was like, "Does

00:44:43.859 --> 00:44:46.720
that mean we get free pizza after the game?"
He was like, "No, that's not what it means."

00:44:46.720 --> 00:44:52.140
And then you could see his brain and he explained,
"No, we're going to be committing on this

00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:54.359
individual." He explained it in English to
me.

00:44:54.359 --> 00:44:57.030
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: Half an hour goes by, he's getting

00:44:57.030 --> 00:45:00.109
ready to tell us the bus schedule. It's all
in Dutch, I have no idea what's going on.

00:45:00.109 --> 00:45:06.170
And I put my hand up and was like, "Does that
mean we get free pizza?" And he's getting

00:45:06.170 --> 00:45:11.660
irritated and then I think it finally clicked
and he was like, "Wait a second, we need to

00:45:11.660 --> 00:45:13.930
be a little bit more patient with this guy."
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:45:13.930 --> 00:45:19.570
Jake Muise: And I tell it as a funny story
because I used that phrase as I went to Indonesia

00:45:19.570 --> 00:45:23.790
and the Maldives and it was this amazing way
for me to ask dumb questions.

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:28.130
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: Because otherwise I was lost.

00:45:28.130 --> 00:45:30.250
And it has translated to one of — and I
know you've talked about this in the past,

00:45:30.250 --> 00:45:35.349
it's translated to one of these small superpowers
that I have where I'm never afraid now to

00:45:35.349 --> 00:45:41.520
ask the dumbest question in front of some
of the smartest people through that practice.

00:45:41.520 --> 00:45:44.819
And it really is, it's a superpower.
Tim Ferriss: I want to highlight this because

00:45:44.819 --> 00:45:49.800
it's really important, right? And I have another
friend, Mike Maples, Jr. And oftentimes when

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:55.069
he wants to ask a question that no one else
is going to ask but it's an important question,

00:45:55.069 --> 00:45:59.530
but people are nervous to ask it for any number
of reasons, and he'll go something along these

00:45:59.530 --> 00:46:05.240
lines. He's from Texas. And he'll be like,
"I'm just a country boy, I move a little slow.

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:12.420
So bear with me, let me ask you." And he's
actually razor sharp, right? But he has that

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:19.130
as a way to wrap a question that needs to
be asked in the same way that you were able

00:46:19.130 --> 00:46:23.720
to buy yourself permission to ask these questions,
which by the way, a lot of the dumb questions

00:46:23.720 --> 00:46:28.850
are on everyone else's mind, not in this particular
case with everyone else who speaks Dutch.

00:46:28.850 --> 00:46:34.030
But there's so many circumstances where there's
some type of pink elephant question, but people

00:46:34.030 --> 00:46:38.640
are nervous to ask it because they don't want
to look stupid or A, B, C, D, or E. But in

00:46:38.640 --> 00:46:41.140
fact, it's a really important question.
Jake Muise: And when I was playing in Indonesia,

00:46:41.140 --> 00:46:46.640
we had a Brazilian guy that spoke even less
English than me. And after my third day of

00:46:46.640 --> 00:46:51.890
asking for free pizza, he came up to me and
he was like, "Thank you so much. I wouldn't

00:46:51.890 --> 00:46:54.450
know what's going on unless you were asking
these questions."

00:46:54.450 --> 00:46:56.849
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: And now it just translates to

00:46:56.849 --> 00:47:01.510
last week, we're building an API, which I
have no idea what the hell this thing — and

00:47:01.510 --> 00:47:07.140
I just kept saying, "I have no idea what you're
talking about." And I had a clue, but I could

00:47:07.140 --> 00:47:09.910
see the blank looks on the Zoom calls.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:47:09.910 --> 00:47:13.690
Jake Muise: Nobody knew what they were talking
about. And it just gets stuff done so much

00:47:13.690 --> 00:47:14.690
faster.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:47:14.690 --> 00:47:18.721
Jake Muise: Yeah, and it's just been this
amazing thing in my life that has worked really,

00:47:18.721 --> 00:47:22.520
really well.
Tim Ferriss: Just to put a bow on the chapter

00:47:22.520 --> 00:47:27.350
of volleyball, so you mentioned that you were
not the Ronaldo of volleyball.

00:47:27.350 --> 00:47:32.099
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: But you ended up taking an oblique

00:47:32.099 --> 00:47:39.640
angle and extending your career related to
