of what I think are interesting features of
things. And a lot of them tie into why Maui

00:48:41.470 --> 00:48:47.690
Nui became my first ever, I mean really if
you look at my portfolio, food investment

00:48:47.690 --> 00:48:56.819
in — when was it? 2019. So it was a while
ago. Part of it was I think there's a myopia

00:48:56.819 --> 00:49:01.940
perhaps with many people who specialize in
some form of early-stage investing, let's

00:49:01.940 --> 00:49:10.520
just say tech, to associate innovation purely
with technological advances in a certain medium

00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:17.170
or in a certain capacity. But I saw a lot
that you guys were doing that reflected a

00:49:17.170 --> 00:49:23.190
level of experimentation and unorthodox thinking
that was super attractive to me that made

00:49:23.190 --> 00:49:27.730
me think it would be in combination with other
things like nutrient density, which we'll

00:49:27.730 --> 00:49:33.319
talk about, attractive. And also, everything
is fucking delicious and I like delicious,

00:49:33.319 --> 00:49:42.730
healthy things. Let's talk about the seven
on, seven off, because this will sound at

00:49:42.730 --> 00:49:46.610
first description to be a little odd for a
lot of folks.

00:49:46.610 --> 00:49:54.810
Jake Muise: Sure. So we have a very unique
schedule for our team members. And all of

00:49:54.810 --> 00:50:00.680
our team members work seven days on, seven
days off. And they get a great compensation

00:50:00.680 --> 00:50:06.220
package, great benefits. And it was originally
designed to deal with flipping back and forth

00:50:06.220 --> 00:50:10.260
from the sleep schedule. All our harvesting
operates at night and we wanted to give our

00:50:10.260 --> 00:50:15.640
harvesters enough space to essentially recover
and feel good and be able to come back to

00:50:15.640 --> 00:50:22.400
work. And when we started to grow, we decided
to keep that system for essentially almost

00:50:22.400 --> 00:50:28.760
all of our field and butcher staff, the vast
majority of who we are. And it's turned into

00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:38.880
this extraordinary benefit that we didn't
see coming. And we did a little experiment

00:50:38.880 --> 00:50:42.910
last year because we wanted to make sure we
were — living in Hawai’i's extraordinarily

00:50:42.910 --> 00:50:49.391
expensive and we were trying to be as responsible
as possible. So we have two full teams, everybody

00:50:49.391 --> 00:50:52.390
that works seven days on and another team
comes in, we essentially work nonstop. We

00:50:52.390 --> 00:50:55.890
have two teams switching back and forth. So
we offered them unlimited overtime. And can

00:50:55.890 --> 00:50:58.040
you guess what happened?
Tim Ferriss: Tell me.

00:50:58.040 --> 00:51:00.819
Jake Muise: Nobody took it.
Tim Ferriss: So explain for a second what

00:51:00.819 --> 00:51:03.059
that would actually mean. What is unlimited
overtime?

00:51:03.059 --> 00:51:07.660
Jake Muise: Yeah. So we said on your seven
days off, you can come in and work every single

00:51:07.660 --> 00:51:10.650
one of those days you work at time and a half,
at an overtime rate.

00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:12.400
Tim Ferriss: And no one did it.
Jake Muise: Nobody did it. And it's just this

00:51:12.400 --> 00:51:13.839
amazing mark —
Tim Ferriss: So what do you make of that?

00:51:13.839 --> 00:51:16.140
Jake Muise: Time —
Tim Ferriss: They were just happy with —

00:51:16.140 --> 00:51:18.119
Jake Muise: A, there's nothing more important
than time.

00:51:18.119 --> 00:51:20.750
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: B, lots of them, it's been this

00:51:20.750 --> 00:51:25.740
really interesting and amazing thing to watch
them. Seven days is a long time.

00:51:25.740 --> 00:51:28.839
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: When we hire somebody, almost

00:51:28.839 --> 00:51:33.010
inevitably their first week off, they call
me and they're like, "What am I supposed to

00:51:33.010 --> 00:51:37.799
do? It's day four." And they're like, "I haven't
had a seven-day vacation in three years, what

00:51:37.799 --> 00:51:40.180
am I supposed to do?" I'm like, "Well, get
used to it."

00:51:40.180 --> 00:51:43.630
Tim Ferriss: Find some hobbies.
Jake Muise: Yeah. And it's been amazing to

00:51:43.630 --> 00:51:49.760
watch them become a better community member
or start a food truck or start their own company

00:51:49.760 --> 00:51:56.210
and they come back. We essentially have 100
percent retention of the people we want. And

00:51:56.210 --> 00:52:00.549
they come back on the start of their seven
days and they are the most extraordinary person

00:52:00.549 --> 00:52:06.890
coming back to us. And then they work amazingly
well for seven days. That age-old adage of

00:52:06.890 --> 00:52:11.600
one person is better than two average is 100
percent true, and we have data to support

00:52:11.600 --> 00:52:13.900
that in how we look at production and stuff
now.

00:52:13.900 --> 00:52:15.450
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: And so the other thing that I

00:52:15.450 --> 00:52:20.911
think is really important for the people we
hire, which are hyper local people, we're

00:52:20.911 --> 00:52:27.210
looking for people of those places, is you're
giving them an opportunity to leverage that

00:52:27.210 --> 00:52:33.049
place and enjoy it. So half of them will just
fish for six days or go hunting or enjoy surfing

00:52:33.049 --> 00:52:38.530
or something else. And it's just this amazing
schedule that really allows them to enjoy

00:52:38.530 --> 00:52:43.800
the place that they are from and live in.
But more importantly, we have an extra extraordinary

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:47.550
group of people. And it just had to roll over
the hill a little bit and combine with the

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:53.549
other things like through COVID when nobody
could hire anybody, we had stacks and stacks

00:52:53.549 --> 00:52:58.339
and stacks of resumes on my desk. It just
has turned into this amazing work environment

00:52:58.339 --> 00:53:02.400
and we've had no lack of essentially efficiency
or production.

00:53:02.400 --> 00:53:11.069
Tim Ferriss: So let's talk about some of the
processes and metrics that inform that output

00:53:11.069 --> 00:53:12.250
and efficiency.
Jake Muise: Sure.

00:53:12.250 --> 00:53:14.060
Tim Ferriss: Because it doesn't happen by
accident.

00:53:14.060 --> 00:53:18.020
Jake Muise: No.
Tim Ferriss: And I've visited your operation

00:53:18.020 --> 00:53:23.780
a few times now and I've always been, this
won't surprise anyone who's followed me for

00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:33.740
a while, I'm always impressed with very finely
tuned tracking. So could you speak to some

00:53:33.740 --> 00:53:38.840
of the metrics that you track and why they're
important?

00:53:38.840 --> 00:53:40.760
Jake Muise: Sure.
Tim Ferriss: And then we'll segue from that

00:53:40.760 --> 00:53:45.819
into let's have you describe what a shift
actually looks like.

00:53:45.819 --> 00:53:47.321
Jake Muise: Sure.
Tim Ferriss: When you're going out at night.

00:53:47.321 --> 00:53:49.390
Jake Muise: Yeah. So we have two —
Tim Ferriss: And you can feel free to switch

00:53:49.390 --> 00:53:51.570
back and forth or do one first and then the
other afterwards.

00:53:51.570 --> 00:53:59.270
Jake Muise: So the harvest side of what we
do, which is really the unique aspect of what

00:53:59.270 --> 00:54:08.880
we've built, is these field harvesting systems
that comply fully with USDA standards, i.e.

00:54:08.880 --> 00:54:12.890
everything that operates in a brick and mortar
we're able to do in the field through a combination

00:54:12.890 --> 00:54:18.089
of forward-looking infrared and these different
software systems that we built. And we were

00:54:18.089 --> 00:54:21.790
only able to create those systems and levels
of efficiency with what you're pointing to

00:54:21.790 --> 00:54:29.320
is an absurd amount of data that we keep.
So we keep track of every mile, every bullet,

00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:36.550
every time we stop, the moon phases, how the
moon phase influences deer, we call it moon-fluence.

00:54:36.550 --> 00:54:40.580
And we have these giant KPIs and sheets that
basically —

00:54:40.580 --> 00:54:44.900
Tim Ferriss: Key performance indicators.
Jake Muise: Yeah, that dictate where we go,

00:54:44.900 --> 00:54:47.940
what we do and then who we put in different
places. Actually, I don't think I've told

00:54:47.940 --> 00:54:52.270
you this, but we just submitted a grant to
NASA. Harvard and MIT, these PhDs at Harvard

00:54:52.270 --> 00:54:57.049
and MIT found out about what we did, found
out we keep an absurd amount of data compared

00:54:57.049 --> 00:55:03.790
to most agricultural companies and NASA has
a grant available for ag companies where they'll

00:55:03.790 --> 00:55:09.099
fly this amazing satellite, comes over Hawai’i
at 10:14, we just finished putting this giant

00:55:09.099 --> 00:55:11.580
grant together, crossing my fingers.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:55:11.580 --> 00:55:16.890
Jake Muise: And it's so detailed that these
amazing people at MIT and Harvard believe

00:55:16.890 --> 00:55:23.640
they'll be able to detect deer with the satellite
and then use the information and data we collect

00:55:23.640 --> 00:55:27.760
every single night on when we're seeing them
and how and how we're interacting with them

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:33.420
to incorporate with the machine learning to
create route planning for us. So they think

00:55:33.420 --> 00:55:38.890
a combination of satellite data and the ground
data that we keep combined will be able to

00:55:38.890 --> 00:55:41.760
tell us on the next day where to go.
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:55:41.760 --> 00:55:46.930
Jake Muise: I was freaking out when they were
explaining all of this and lots of dumb questions

00:55:46.930 --> 00:55:53.080
that went along with it, yeah. But they were
shocked at the amount of data we keep. And

00:55:53.080 --> 00:55:58.080
they even got to see we've run lots of different
experiments of how we collect data. And I

00:55:58.080 --> 00:56:03.869
think maybe what you're pointing to is just
this constant iteration of we're going to

00:56:03.869 --> 00:56:08.470
try this thing, we're going to keep data.
I think there's that great saying, "Measure

00:56:08.470 --> 00:56:11.950
what matters." If you don't measure it, you
won't know if it's actually worked and we

00:56:11.950 --> 00:56:17.760
are just constantly changing and measuring
things. And so we've actually taken that philosophy

00:56:17.760 --> 00:56:25.210
into our butcher facility, which is a very
well-known industry with well-known inputs

00:56:25.210 --> 00:56:33.299
and production efficiencies. And we're crushing
those numbers and we're just doing it through

00:56:33.299 --> 00:56:39.190
constant iteration. Doesn't matter if the
angle of a table or this machine or the different

00:56:39.190 --> 00:56:43.540
knife that we use, we are constantly iterating
and constantly collecting data to see are

00:56:43.540 --> 00:56:46.290
we producing a better product faster with
more quality?

00:56:46.290 --> 00:56:50.109
Tim Ferriss: So let me come back to the USDA.
Jake Muise: Sure.

00:56:50.109 --> 00:56:56.319
Tim Ferriss: Or maybe not directly USDA, but
comparables. And then we'll go from there

00:56:56.319 --> 00:57:02.771
to a sample night, what the run of show looks
like in the process. And the reason I want

00:57:02.771 --> 00:57:11.200
to talk about these two things is that in
the field, things are harder to control than

00:57:11.200 --> 00:57:17.991
you would find in a facility where you're
using, say, cattle chutes and so on. What

00:57:17.991 --> 00:57:22.910
does your, and people are not going to like
some of these words, so apologies to those

00:57:22.910 --> 00:57:28.550
people, but I think it's fascinating and important
to cover these things, what does your, say,

00:57:28.550 --> 00:57:37.790
kill efficiency or your measurements of humane
kill look like compared to a conventional

00:57:37.790 --> 00:57:39.369
facility?
Jake Muise: Great question.

00:57:39.369 --> 00:57:44.119
Tim Ferriss: And the reason I wanted to talk
about this, there are quite a few, but one

00:57:44.119 --> 00:57:52.589
is that I know that a decent portion of your
customer base is vegans or vegetarians who

00:57:52.589 --> 00:57:57.270
make a sole allowance for Maui Nui venison.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:57:57.270 --> 00:58:03.460
Tim Ferriss: Which is maybe a head scratcher
for a lot of people. So let's talk about your

00:58:03.460 --> 00:58:14.440
field efficiency compared to what most people
are indirectly experiencing when they buy

00:58:14.440 --> 00:58:19.069
something just wrapped in styrofoam at their
local grocery store.

00:58:19.069 --> 00:58:26.030
Jake Muise: Sure. Part of the systems we had
to build were we had to follow the Federal

00:58:26.030 --> 00:58:31.830
Meat Inspection Act, which the USDA is present
to make sure that animal is supposed to be

00:58:31.830 --> 00:58:37.190
harvested humanely and then that ultimately,
after that animal is processed, make sure

00:58:37.190 --> 00:58:43.109
it's safe to eat. That's their two primary
functions. And I know this is hard for people

00:58:43.109 --> 00:58:49.000
to hear, but if you eat meat like it's what
it is, in a typical brick and mortar facility,

00:58:49.000 --> 00:58:54.660
that animal is coming in on a trailer moving
through a chute system going into a press

00:58:54.660 --> 00:59:00.349
most often. So that animal is completely stationary
and then the language is called rendered,

00:59:00.349 --> 00:59:04.420
but it's then essentially shot in the head.
So we had to follow those exact same rules

00:59:04.420 --> 00:59:10.430
in the field and with no control of the animal.
Tim Ferriss: The rules meaning humane, safe

00:59:10.430 --> 00:59:11.750
to eat.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

00:59:11.750 --> 00:59:16.990
Tim Ferriss: But not following that process.
Jake Muise: But not following that process,

00:59:16.990 --> 00:59:23.130
yeah. So we don't interact with these animals
at all prior to that rendering or harvesting

00:59:23.130 --> 00:59:30.240
process. They're entirely wild. And what we
developed was a particular shooting system

00:59:30.240 --> 00:59:36.630
that allows and/or standardized our shooters
to miss on purpose 30 percent of the time.

00:59:36.630 --> 00:59:43.110
And that sounds weird, but what it is is we
are essentially making sure every shot that

00:59:43.110 --> 00:59:49.039
we hit that animal is perfect and there's
never any outside injuries. And to do that,

00:59:49.039 --> 00:59:54.270
if you shoot a two-inch group at 200 yards,
which is difficult to do, and you aim at the

00:59:54.270 --> 01:00:00.540
very, very top or tip of a head, you are going
to miss about 30 percent of the time. And

01:00:00.540 --> 01:00:02.970
so we actually operate —
Tim Ferriss: So just to —

01:00:02.970 --> 01:00:06.039
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Paint a visual for people, so

01:00:06.039 --> 01:00:09.010
two inches, it's a little bit bigger than,
say, a silver dollar.

01:00:09.010 --> 01:00:12.480
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So imagine you have something

01:00:12.480 --> 01:00:16.820
that's slightly bigger than a silver dollar
and you put it 600 feet away, you need to

01:00:16.820 --> 01:00:19.930
put every shot inside that silver dollar.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

01:00:19.930 --> 01:00:22.450
Tim Ferriss: That's what we're talking about.
Jake Muise: Yeah. And then you need to make

01:00:22.450 --> 01:00:30.220
sure your aim point is in a place that never
can create injury, because if you miss high,

01:00:30.220 --> 01:00:33.040
nothing happens. That animal runs away.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:00:33.040 --> 01:00:39.400
Jake Muise: And so we actually operate at
about a 99.9 percent rendering efficiency.

01:00:39.400 --> 01:00:42.630
Tim Ferriss: And what is the brick and mortar
average?

01:00:42.630 --> 01:00:50.180
Jake Muise: 98 off of thousands and thousands
of animals. So we were able to meet and exceed

01:00:50.180 --> 01:00:56.010
those guidelines or those rules.
Tim Ferriss: In the wild with animals moving

01:00:56.010 --> 01:00:57.910
around.
Jake Muise: Yeah, with a combination of — so

01:00:57.910 --> 01:01:02.780
you can imagine, and this maybe leads into
what a night in the field looks like, but

01:01:02.780 --> 01:01:10.299
you have these Mad Max-looking UTVs with forward-looking
infrared screens essentially attached. The

01:01:10.299 --> 01:01:15.950
USDA inspector is sitting right beside you
the entire time. And the technology is so

01:01:15.950 --> 01:01:21.589
good that the heat, the friction the bullet
makes passing through the air, the USDA inspector's

01:01:21.589 --> 01:01:26.411
actually able to see that bullet that took
the head of every single animal. And it's

01:01:26.411 --> 01:01:30.550
just an extraordinary process where the animal
is unaware that you're there. And so it is

01:01:30.550 --> 01:01:34.079
truly, truly wild until the second harvest
and then dies immediately.

01:01:34.079 --> 01:01:39.619
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: And so which comes into play for

01:01:39.619 --> 01:01:43.790
nutrient density, there is no stress.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, they're not flooded with

01:01:43.790 --> 01:01:45.930
—
Jake Muise: Prior to harvesting. And I think

01:01:45.930 --> 01:01:51.130
when we get about a message a week from a
vegan that says, "I'm finally able to come

01:01:51.130 --> 01:01:57.319
back to eating meat," because they understand
why the animal has to be managed and why that

01:01:57.319 --> 01:02:03.109
animal's dying, and then they agree and connect
strongly with that process. It's truly the

01:02:03.109 --> 01:02:11.240
only stress-free harvesting or slaughter of
an animal within our food system.

01:02:11.240 --> 01:02:15.510
Tim Ferriss: Could you talk for a second about
why this is so rare, right? Because people

01:02:15.510 --> 01:02:19.180
might think, "Well can't you just go hunting
and sell the meat?" You cannot do that.

01:02:19.180 --> 01:02:21.720
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So what makes you able to do

01:02:21.720 --> 01:02:22.720
this?
Jake Muise: Yeah, great question.

01:02:22.720 --> 01:02:23.920
Tim Ferriss: For people who don't have that
background.

01:02:23.920 --> 01:02:33.170
Jake Muise: So an elk in Montana is a native
species and it is owned by the public. So

01:02:33.170 --> 01:02:38.440
it's on public land. It's on public land.
Even on private land, that animal, the management

01:02:38.440 --> 01:02:44.030
of that animal is dictated by the state. It
is still owned by the people, right? So an

01:02:44.030 --> 01:02:54.660
invasive species on private land in Hawai’i
is the liability and ownership of the landowner.

01:02:54.660 --> 01:03:00.029
So it is a very unique situation in which
you can actually do this process legally.

01:03:00.029 --> 01:03:06.089
And it's only in the instance where it is
an invasive species on private land. We are

01:03:06.089 --> 01:03:13.049
not allowed to operate on public land in Hawai’i.
Tim Ferriss: And I bring this up, I mean obviously,

01:03:13.049 --> 01:03:18.740
look, I love Maui Nui. I'm an investor, so
of course I'm biased. But it all came down

01:03:18.740 --> 01:03:21.150
to, and I think we were initially introduced
by Peter Attia.

01:03:21.150 --> 01:03:24.980
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: The quality, just this pure quality

01:03:24.980 --> 01:03:29.200
and nutrient density of the product for me,
right? That's what it came down to. But I

01:03:29.200 --> 01:03:38.890
think as means of just educating people about
game meat and the sale of game meat in general,

01:03:38.890 --> 01:03:46.140
or let's just call it atypical proteins like
venison, elk, and so on. How much of the venison,

01:03:46.140 --> 01:03:51.150
elk, et cetera, that people might buy at a
Whole Foods or something is farmed?

01:03:51.150 --> 01:03:53.490
Jake Muise: 100 percent.
Tim Ferriss: 100 percent. So I bring this

01:03:53.490 --> 01:03:58.880
up just to say that people say you are what
you eat. That is true, but you're also what

01:03:58.880 --> 01:04:06.540
you eat ate. So if an animal, let's say deer
or an elk, is being fed, who knows, corn feed

01:04:06.540 --> 01:04:10.740
and all sorts of stuff, and given antibiotics,
potentially, it's very different from something

01:04:10.740 --> 01:04:16.000
that is wild-harvested. So I don't want to
skip too far ahead. Let's talk about a night

01:04:16.000 --> 01:04:20.760
out, just what that looks like, and then we'll
talk about nutrient profile.

01:04:20.760 --> 01:04:31.340
Jake Muise: You can imagine a bunch of hyper
local Hawaiians, who I love, getting up, rolling

01:04:31.340 --> 01:04:36.380
out of bed. We all sleep on site. We've got
all these cubby holes for them to sleep in.

01:04:36.380 --> 01:04:39.100
We all sleep on site. We're getting up, making
—

01:04:39.100 --> 01:04:42.150
Tim Ferriss: 10:00 p.m.
Jake Muise: 10:00 p.m. Making dinner and breakfast

01:04:42.150 --> 01:04:47.349
together. Some of them are diesel engines.
You've really got to shake them to get going.

01:04:47.349 --> 01:04:53.119
And we are constantly moving, so we have mobile
facilities where we're moving from site to

01:04:53.119 --> 01:04:57.210
site to do this process. We have these primary
sites on all of the different ranches that

01:04:57.210 --> 01:05:02.390
we work with. So the first thing we're looking
at each evening is moving our mobile facilities

01:05:02.390 --> 01:05:06.530
to another site to get closer to the deer.
And the reason is, one of the other rules

01:05:06.530 --> 01:05:14.180
we have to follow is after that animal is
rendered, it has to be back to the facility,

01:05:14.180 --> 01:05:17.520
cleaned, processed, without a single hair
on it, in an hour.

01:05:17.520 --> 01:05:25.059
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. It's pretty mind-boggling.
So we'll also link to a video that shows snippets

01:05:25.059 --> 01:05:33.420
of this that's on YouTube. Because if people
think about that, I mean, it's pretty mind-boggling

01:05:33.420 --> 01:05:34.420
to think about it.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So we're asking our —

01:05:34.420 --> 01:05:35.420
Tim Ferriss: And maybe you can walk through
how that's accomplished.

01:05:35.420 --> 01:05:41.150
Jake Muise: Yeah. So we're asking our team
members to be snipers, rally car drivers,

01:05:41.150 --> 01:05:47.809
CrossFit athletes, butchers. The people we
have on our teams just have these extraordinary

01:05:47.809 --> 01:05:52.029
skill sets. It's the funnest thing to watch
a guy in his first week.

01:05:52.029 --> 01:05:55.190
Tim Ferriss: Access to your decathletes.
Jake Muise: Yeah. We have to pick up every

01:05:55.190 --> 01:05:59.290
single one of these animals and carry them
on our backs, because you're not allowed to

01:05:59.290 --> 01:06:05.560
drag them. You can either introduce contaminants
and/or bruise the meat. And it's actually

01:06:05.560 --> 01:06:10.859
one of my favorite parts of the night is it
is the complete opposite of the rest of the

01:06:10.859 --> 01:06:16.809
meat industry. After you've killed this animal
for food, you're carrying this animal on your

01:06:16.809 --> 01:06:20.819
back into its next stage of life with the
food. And there's an individual connection

01:06:20.819 --> 01:06:26.599
with each one of these animals. And it's something
that every guy on our team really takes pride

01:06:26.599 --> 01:06:31.710
in, is being able to pick up these giant,
250-pound animals across razor sharp lava

01:06:31.710 --> 01:06:34.660
or all super —
Tim Ferriss: 250. That's a big boy.

01:06:34.660 --> 01:06:39.120
Jake Muise: You have these giant bucks. Anyway,
we're moving mobile slaughter facilities and

01:06:39.120 --> 01:06:43.860
we're getting all set up in stage and you've
got these Mad Max UTVs. And everything is

01:06:43.860 --> 01:06:49.160
getting ready for what we call the performance
period. And the USDA shows up and it is essentially

01:06:49.160 --> 01:06:55.280
game time. You have a finite period of time,
which for a long time was only three hours.

01:06:55.280 --> 01:07:01.160
So you have all of the variability of a wild
animal and you have to make it all work in

01:07:01.160 --> 01:07:07.741
a three-hour period. Your entire business
comes down to — your mission business, how

01:07:07.741 --> 01:07:12.550
you operate, comes down to managing variances
in a three-hour period.

01:07:12.550 --> 01:07:18.990
So nobody walks. Everybody drives faster than
they'd like to. And we've never had more than

01:07:18.990 --> 01:07:25.060
a cut finger, knock on wood. And we expect
just extraordinary people and athletes to

01:07:25.060 --> 01:07:30.549
come work with us. We have had lots of people
that have come and been with us for a week

01:07:30.549 --> 01:07:38.299
and said, "I can't do this. I can't do this
every night. It's impossible." So USDA comes,

01:07:38.299 --> 01:07:42.730
we have this very defined performance period
where we're going really, really fast under

01:07:42.730 --> 01:07:47.569
control and getting as many animals as we
can, getting them back and processing them.

01:07:47.569 --> 01:07:51.119
And then before you know it, the sun has come
up and the night is over.

01:07:51.119 --> 01:07:55.319
Tim Ferriss: Now, just so we understand some
of the tools of the trade that go into this,

01:07:55.319 --> 01:08:01.400
so you're using night vision scopes? How is
the technology layered to make this work?

01:08:01.400 --> 01:08:05.119
Jake Muise: So the first piece of technology
that will start the night is these forward-looking

01:08:05.119 --> 01:08:10.720
infrared drones. So a drone is going in there
to give us a picture of a couple thousand

01:08:10.720 --> 01:08:17.480
acres to say deer are going to be in these
areas as we get started. The forward-looking

01:08:17.480 --> 01:08:22.239
infrared binocular system that we use, I can
tell you the difference between a goat and

01:08:22.239 --> 01:08:31.109
a deer at seven miles. So at 150 yards, you
can see every hair, any abscess. The USDA

01:08:31.109 --> 01:08:35.609
being able to say that animal is healthy is
achieved through that technology. And there's

01:08:35.609 --> 01:08:40.859
no lights on. It's completely black. Deer
has no idea what's going on as they're being

01:08:40.859 --> 01:08:48.710
evaluated by the USDA. And then the shooters
also have forward-looking infrared scopes.

01:08:48.710 --> 01:08:53.290
So you never have to introduce light into
the scenario to scare them. And that's what

01:08:53.290 --> 01:08:57.780
allows you to harvest enough animals every
night to make it work. So you come up over

01:08:57.780 --> 01:09:02.750
a hill, you see a couple deer, all of the
lights go off, all of this forward-looking

01:09:02.750 --> 01:09:06.060
infrared equipment comes on instantly so the
USDA is able to view it.

01:09:06.060 --> 01:09:10.500
He's able to look at those five animals and
say, "Yep, you're able to take those animals."

01:09:10.500 --> 01:09:16.630
And we need to wait. He has to verbally say
those things. Oftentimes you can shoot several

01:09:16.630 --> 01:09:21.710
of those animals because they don't actually
know what's going on. It's the middle of the

01:09:21.710 --> 01:09:26.150
night. And the minute they don't feel safe,
they just run. So you know you're still harvesting

01:09:26.150 --> 01:09:29.290
them under stress-free conditions if they're
just standing there. Half the time, they'll

01:09:29.290 --> 01:09:36.449
go back to feeding. Animals are rendered properly,
they go down immediately. And as soon as our

01:09:36.449 --> 01:09:43.940
shooter says "Clear," and the USDA says "Clear,"
people are sprinting into the bushes to find

01:09:43.940 --> 01:09:47.190
these animals. Again, it's the middle of the
night. If the grass is four feet tall, we

01:09:47.190 --> 01:09:53.359
have laser pointer systems that come off of
the UTVs to show them the right direction.

01:09:53.359 --> 01:09:58.150
And animal gets picked up, brought back to
the UTV, and then we'll collect a couple of

01:09:58.150 --> 01:10:03.140
them. We have a roving team, they're called.
So we have a team of rovers that essentially

01:10:03.140 --> 01:10:07.000
are the Mad Max team, where they'll collect
a couple deer and then they'll move as fast

01:10:07.000 --> 01:10:12.350
as they can back to where the mobile slaughter
and processing facilities are stationed. And

01:10:12.350 --> 01:10:18.000
so they're just cycling back and forth between
the harvesting team and the processing teams.

01:10:18.000 --> 01:10:23.990
So it's just this intricate dance. And this
is all at night on — this is not paved roads,

01:10:23.990 --> 01:10:29.280
these are rough off-road scenarios. And so
one of my favorite things to do is the first

01:10:29.280 --> 01:10:36.350
week we train somebody, we have a course that
they have to run. And we let them run the

01:10:36.350 --> 01:10:40.820
course once, and it typically takes like seven
minutes. And we're like, "We need you to do

01:10:40.820 --> 01:10:46.281
that in three minutes." And they're just like,
"What?" "We need you to do that in three minutes

01:10:46.281 --> 01:10:52.400
or you're not going to get back in time."
And so it's always fun to sit in that seat.

01:10:52.400 --> 01:10:57.480
And we have these very specialized UTVs that
are essentially impossible to tip over. But

01:10:57.480 --> 01:11:04.320
it is so fun to watch these amazing local
guys develop all these incredible skill sets.

01:11:04.320 --> 01:11:10.750
And every morning the sun comes up and everybody's
just grinning ear to ear. It very much feels

01:11:10.750 --> 01:11:14.540
like a sports team. And obviously with my
history, I run it kind of like a sports team

01:11:14.540 --> 01:11:19.180
and the coach. And just an amazing experience
to do one of these hunts.

01:11:19.180 --> 01:11:24.650
Tim Ferriss: So just to define terms also,
if people don't know what UTV means, it's

01:11:24.650 --> 01:11:27.360
kind of like a super tactical Mad Max golf
cart.

01:11:27.360 --> 01:11:29.270
Jake Muise: There you go.
Tim Ferriss: That's a bit larger. Right?

01:11:29.270 --> 01:11:32.170
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So people may have seen — Polaris

01:11:32.170 --> 01:11:37.590
is one brand name, there are many others.
There are other terms used to refer to these

01:11:37.590 --> 01:11:46.860
things, but that's what we're talking about.
So what are some of the other keys to selecting

01:11:46.860 --> 01:11:55.330
or building a team like you have built? Because
you're asking a lot of these people. And it's

01:11:55.330 --> 01:12:04.230
an unusual work culture. So what are some
of your frameworks, systems, criteria for

01:12:04.230 --> 01:12:10.090
hiring, but also training once people are
in the position?

01:12:10.090 --> 01:12:15.520
Jake Muise: I mean, great people already being
there to train great people really, really

01:12:15.520 --> 01:12:21.820
goes a long way. And we can talk about that
system we built later, if you want, but the

01:12:21.820 --> 01:12:25.770
communication and people being patient is
probably the most important thing. You have

01:12:25.770 --> 01:12:30.380
to imagine, you're doing almost everything
through radios so you sound like Kermit the

01:12:30.380 --> 01:12:36.290
Frog all night long. And you only catch half
of it sometimes, but you're moving really

01:12:36.290 --> 01:12:41.909
fast and the expectation is you keep moving
fast. And so somebody has to be patient enough

01:12:41.909 --> 01:12:45.941
when, "Oh, I didn't get that message," or,
"I didn't know what's it like." Communication

01:12:45.941 --> 01:12:52.290
is so key when you can't see body language,
you can't hear properly. And everybody's operating

01:12:52.290 --> 01:12:58.909
out of a headlamp, so you have this little
10-degree view of what you can see through

01:12:58.909 --> 01:13:02.430
light. And I'm sure it's the most critical
thing in a lot of businesses, but when you're

01:13:02.430 --> 01:13:08.270
operating like that at night, it just becomes
this amplified skill that if you don't have,

01:13:08.270 --> 01:13:10.850
we get on it so fast.
Tim Ferriss: And communication, how do you

01:13:10.850 --> 01:13:12.541
get on it?
Jake Muise: So early on, I've never had a

01:13:12.541 --> 01:13:20.790
hard time talking, having hard conversations.
So I would talk to people about tone and approaches

01:13:20.790 --> 01:13:26.489
and different words they could use, like reminder
instead of, "Get this thing done." We really

01:13:26.489 --> 01:13:32.180
focused on individual language. And then we
basically built a language of harvesting.

01:13:32.180 --> 01:13:34.470
So we have all of these key words. So we're
rolling, we have all these key words that

01:13:34.470 --> 01:13:39.520
we use so people don't get confused with what's
going on. So we've created our own harvest

01:13:39.520 --> 01:13:46.290
language that allows people not to get confused
when we're going really fast. And then ultimately

01:13:46.290 --> 01:13:52.790
we built a system called HHS, which stands
for Humble, Hungry, Smart and is largely based

01:13:52.790 --> 01:13:54.590
on —
Tim Ferriss: HPA. What was the first one?

01:13:54.590 --> 01:13:56.670
Jake Muise: Humble, Hungry, Smart.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Gotcha. So [inaudible]

01:13:56.670 --> 01:13:58.620
Canadian.
Jake Muise: Yeah. And it's largely based on

01:13:58.620 --> 01:14:05.910
a book that Patrick Lencioni wrote called
The Ideal Team Player. And it is a peer-to-peer

01:14:05.910 --> 01:14:14.650
evaluation system that measures humility,
work ethic and emotional intelligence. And

01:14:14.650 --> 01:14:20.670
it became so important for us to have the
right people. The biggest risk we have every

01:14:20.670 --> 01:14:25.120
single night is safety. And it became so critically
important to have the right people and the

01:14:25.120 --> 01:14:29.950
right personalities that we had to build a
system to measure those personalities.

01:14:29.950 --> 01:14:32.360
Tim Ferriss: And that's in the recruiting
vetting process?

01:14:32.360 --> 01:14:34.120
Jake Muise: Oh, yeah.
Tim Ferriss: How do you measure those things?

01:14:34.120 --> 01:14:36.409
Humility.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So we created essentially

01:14:36.409 --> 01:14:43.340
a scorecard. It has six questions per category,
so six for humility, six for work ethic, six

01:14:43.340 --> 01:14:49.260
for emotional intelligence. Questions like
do the energy they bring every night, is it

01:14:49.260 --> 01:14:54.860
consistent, as in it positive? So we talk
about body language and emotional intelligence

01:14:54.860 --> 01:14:58.090
and all these different things within these
questions. And I'm happy to provide this thing.

01:14:58.090 --> 01:15:04.140
And we use it in three ways. We give it to
the person on the second interview when we're

01:15:04.140 --> 01:15:10.300
hiring somebody. So we say, "Here's the HHS
system. This is the only thing you're accountable

01:15:10.300 --> 01:15:16.210
to in your first month." And two things happen.
They read it and they go — or actually they

01:15:16.210 --> 01:15:21.190
just don't call back.
And I've made a ton of hiring mistakes in

01:15:21.190 --> 01:15:27.699
the past where you get past this honeymoon
phase and people turn into grouches and there's

01:15:27.699 --> 01:15:31.400
people's personalities involved. And I remember
a guy that we were going to hire that was

01:15:31.400 --> 01:15:34.700
a brilliant electrical engineer who wanted
to quit his job, come work with us, because

01:15:34.700 --> 01:15:37.699
he heard about the seven and seven schedule
and thought it'd be the best thing ever. And

01:15:37.699 --> 01:15:43.710
then he read it and he didn't call me back.
And I really wanted to hire him, and I called

01:15:43.710 --> 01:15:47.290
him back and I said, "Any reason you didn't
call me back?" He's like, "I have terrible

01:15:47.290 --> 01:15:52.949
body language and I'm not willing to quit
my job and take the chance within the first

01:15:52.949 --> 01:15:59.460
month that I get fired for bad body language."
Because we score them. If you're an A, we

01:15:59.460 --> 01:16:04.730
celebrate and figure out a way to reward you.
If you're a B, we find immediate improvements

01:16:04.730 --> 01:16:08.130
that you need to make within some of these
categories. And if you are a C, we let you

01:16:08.130 --> 01:16:12.420
go on the spot, no questions asked.
Tim Ferriss: And you set that expectation

01:16:12.420 --> 01:16:14.330
up front.
Jake Muise: We set that in the interview.

01:16:14.330 --> 01:16:20.170
They have to agree to this system coming in.
They're evaluated by their entire team, including

01:16:20.170 --> 01:16:24.790
them doing a self eval, which is a part of
the overall score. They get to grade themselves.

01:16:24.790 --> 01:16:32.690
Tim Ferriss: And the team, are they sent on
a test evening prior to hiring, or I guess

01:16:32.690 --> 01:16:34.580
they just know that once they start going
out —

01:16:34.580 --> 01:16:38.360
Jake Muise: Yeah. So we call them tryouts.
But when somebody comes to try out with us,

01:16:38.360 --> 01:16:43.580
which means we give them a month, we hire
them on, but we call them a tryout. And at

01:16:43.580 --> 01:16:48.450
the end of that tryout, though, which is typically
a one-month period, they get graded by their

01:16:48.450 --> 01:16:52.610
entire team. And they know. It's this really
tense moment where —

01:16:52.610 --> 01:16:54.411
Tim Ferriss: How many people are on a given
team?

01:16:54.411 --> 01:16:56.060
Jake Muise: Eight to 10.
Tim Ferriss: Got it. Yeah.

01:16:56.060 --> 01:16:59.391
Jake Muise: So it's a good average. So if
somebody —

01:16:59.391 --> 01:17:01.540
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, if somebody's got a bug
up their ass about somebody —

01:17:01.540 --> 01:17:02.640
Jake Muise: It doesn't matter.
Tim Ferriss: It's just one person.

01:17:02.640 --> 01:17:07.150
Jake Muise: It always ends up being a great
average and it always ends up being a great

01:17:07.150 --> 01:17:14.830
measure of that person. And it's been this
extraordinary filter for hiring. Asshole is

01:17:14.830 --> 01:17:22.250
the wrong word, but when we figured out how
to use that system, we now grade every person

01:17:22.250 --> 01:17:27.679
at their first month. We grade every single
person quarterly, including me. Every single

01:17:27.679 --> 01:17:33.429
person gets graded. And there's questions
on there like, "Do they try or ask to do more

01:17:33.429 --> 01:17:40.230
than is required of them every day?" And it's
been so amazing to see the mistakes I make

01:17:40.230 --> 01:17:43.230
in when we're hiring new people or moving
people around.

01:17:43.230 --> 01:17:48.660
A great example is I moved a couple people
from a field position into a management position,

01:17:48.660 --> 01:17:53.270
and then all of a sudden their work ethic
score started coming down. And it wasn't because

01:17:53.270 --> 01:17:56.889
they weren't working any harder, it was I
didn't do a good job defining to the team

01:17:56.889 --> 01:18:01.250
what their new responsibilities were. So they
saw them sitting on a computer and doing these

01:18:01.250 --> 01:18:05.400
things, and they're like, "Well, they're not
in the field helping us." And so it's just

01:18:05.400 --> 01:18:11.440
this amazing quarterly exercise that just
pulls out all of the tension within your teams

01:18:11.440 --> 01:18:17.880
and creates framework for people to address
those tensions. And then ultimately what's

01:18:17.880 --> 01:18:21.430
amazing is to watch people grow.
Tim Ferriss: How do you give feedback? Let's

01:18:21.430 --> 01:18:23.020
say they come back and they've got a bunch
of Bs.

01:18:23.020 --> 01:18:24.380
Jake Muise: Yep. So we —
Tim Ferriss: What's the big boss do?

01:18:24.380 --> 01:18:26.140
Jake Muise: Yeah. So we sit them down and
we say —

01:18:26.140 --> 01:18:28.930
Tim Ferriss: Is that the royal we or is it
just you?

01:18:28.930 --> 01:18:30.110
Jake Muise: No, no.
Tim Ferriss: It's multiple people.

01:18:30.110 --> 01:18:36.441
Jake Muise: Yeah, it's me and the two or three
other harvest managers. Great example. One

01:18:36.441 --> 01:18:42.969
of the questions we ask talks about are they
genuinely happy to see their teammates succeeding?

01:18:42.969 --> 01:18:46.429
Because safety is such an important part of
what we do, when we bring somebody in that's

01:18:46.429 --> 01:18:52.211
more talented, just like a sports team, moves
the best people into the best positions, we

01:18:52.211 --> 01:18:57.390
immediately move people around positions based
on their skillsets. So somebody has to be

01:18:57.390 --> 01:19:02.110
genuinely happy to train somebody that may
replace them in a role that they may enjoy

01:19:02.110 --> 01:19:04.920
more.
Tim Ferriss: They used to be the right striker

01:19:04.920 --> 01:19:06.010
or whatever.
Jake Muise: Exactly.

01:19:06.010 --> 01:19:09.330
Tim Ferriss: And they just got replaced.
Jake Muise: And it's amazing to watch somebody

01:19:09.330 --> 01:19:13.409
that really wants to be there because they
find purpose and they really love the schedule

01:19:13.409 --> 01:19:19.060
and they know the impact that they're having
to our community have to make the decision

01:19:19.060 --> 01:19:24.381
to be better for their teammate every night,
to be celebrating that person's growth, even

01:19:24.381 --> 01:19:28.940
though it's potentially coming at the cost
of something that they enjoy. And so there's

01:19:28.940 --> 01:19:29.940
these —
Tim Ferriss: That's hard.

01:19:29.940 --> 01:19:31.880
Jake Muise: Oh.
Tim Ferriss: I mean, I'm not saying it's unreasonable,

01:19:31.880 --> 01:19:36.520
but I mean, that's asking a lot of a lot of
people. I mean, I don't know if I would — if

01:19:36.520 --> 01:19:39.090
I'm being honest with myself, I think that'd
be hard.

01:19:39.090 --> 01:19:45.690
Jake Muise: Here's the thing. It's been such
a amazing exercise, with lots of iterations.

01:19:45.690 --> 01:19:52.010
The first three iterations I made so many
grown men cry and I felt so bad.

01:19:52.010 --> 01:19:55.239
Tim Ferriss: Was that your delivery or the
measurement? Were you just like, "Hey, listen,

01:19:55.239 --> 01:19:57.921
fuckface?"
Jake Muise: It was just a mess. The measurement

01:19:57.921 --> 01:19:59.230
was wrong.
Tim Ferriss: "You can sink or swim."

01:19:59.230 --> 01:20:04.860
Jake Muise: No, you're getting evaluated by
your peers on your personality and the value

01:20:04.860 --> 01:20:12.250
it's bringing to your team. And you have to
sit down quarterly and be told, "Your humility

01:20:12.250 --> 01:20:16.270
is not good enough for this team."
Tim Ferriss: I assume that the responses are

01:20:16.270 --> 01:20:18.810
all anonymized.
Jake Muise: Yeah, everything's anonymized

01:20:18.810 --> 01:20:22.090
and then averaged.
Tim Ferriss: For what it's worth, I've done

01:20:22.090 --> 01:20:27.340
what's called a 360 interview. And I know
people who as executives or founders have

01:20:27.340 --> 01:20:35.810
had these done. And without exception, myself
included, every time that I spoke to somebody

01:20:35.810 --> 01:20:39.130
who's experienced this for the first time,
they're like, "I went and then sat in my car

01:20:39.130 --> 01:20:47.790
and I basically had a nervous breakdown crisis
of meaning. What do I do?" These are names

01:20:47.790 --> 01:20:50.770
everybody would recognize, but they were just
like, "Holy shit."

01:20:50.770 --> 01:20:57.000
Jake Muise: Yeah. The first time we did it
with a large enough team that I included myself

01:20:57.000 --> 01:21:00.260
in it, because we were just so small early
on. It was I think the third iteration. I

01:21:00.260 --> 01:21:04.530
was like, "I need to be a part of this." And
I got all of the feedback back. I was just

01:21:04.530 --> 01:21:11.800
like, "Oh, my God." But if we really want
to build extraordinary teams, I realized my

01:21:11.800 --> 01:21:19.870
approach to some of our conversations had
to be so much better and nuanced to make them

01:21:19.870 --> 01:21:23.940
better. And it wasn't the right approach.
And you learn all of this. You end up reading

01:21:23.940 --> 01:21:28.330
this thing like braille after doing it. I've
done it hundreds of times now.

01:21:28.330 --> 01:21:31.300
Tim Ferriss: And with repetition, I imagine.
It's like exercise, right? It's like, okay,

01:21:31.300 --> 01:21:34.770
if you're going to do apply metrics once a
year, you are going to be very, very, very

01:21:34.770 --> 01:21:38.560
sore. And you might even hurt yourself. But
if you're doing it routinely it changes.

01:21:38.560 --> 01:21:43.680
Jake Muise: But it was amazing to see what
happened. We built this system because we

01:21:43.680 --> 01:21:48.980
knew we had to go from like eight people to
45 in a really short period of time to hit

01:21:48.980 --> 01:21:54.890
our mission goals about a year and a half
ago. And I had made poor hiring decisions

01:21:54.890 --> 01:22:02.420
in the past, and they were mostly personality-based,
or that person was operating amazing when

01:22:02.420 --> 01:22:05.949
I was around, but the minute I left, they
turned into a different person. And then there's

01:22:05.949 --> 01:22:12.440
this he said, she said game that this completely
erases all of that. Because it's anonymous

01:22:12.440 --> 01:22:19.260
team scoring and the manager doesn't have
a unweighted vote whether that person stays

01:22:19.260 --> 01:22:26.350
around. And what ended up happening is that
HHS program started attracting people. They

01:22:26.350 --> 01:22:30.199
started hearing about this accountability
process to ensure —

01:22:30.199 --> 01:22:33.710
Tim Ferriss: You're attracting better fits.
Jake Muise: Oh, yeah.

01:22:33.710 --> 01:22:38.830
Tim Ferriss: So you're not saying, "Hey, fuckface"
in the fourth, fifth, 10th, 20th iteration.

01:22:38.830 --> 01:22:45.360
What's the language that you use if somebody
has growth opportunities, let's call them?

01:22:45.360 --> 01:22:51.190
Jake Muise: Great. It's really specific to
which of the 24 or which of the 18 categories

01:22:51.190 --> 01:22:53.840
they're struggling in.
Tim Ferriss: But how does the meeting start?

01:22:53.840 --> 01:22:57.679
Jake Muise: Okay. So we sit down and we're
going to give them paper and we say, "Okay,

01:22:57.679 --> 01:23:00.170
you're a B-minus."
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. "Tim, we put a 250-pound

01:23:00.170 --> 01:23:03.040
animal on your back. You crumpled into an
origami crane and you couldn't get up."

01:23:03.040 --> 01:23:07.810
Jake Muise: So you're a B-minus. And then
we celebrate, first, some of the categories

01:23:07.810 --> 01:23:12.300
that they're doing really well in. Some of
the categories that point to professionalism

01:23:12.300 --> 01:23:16.131
or energy or all these different things we
celebrate right away because each one of the

01:23:16.131 --> 01:23:20.840
24 segments have different scores within them
that have been averaged throughout their team.

01:23:20.840 --> 01:23:27.110
And then we address the ones that they're
like a C in. And a great example, a lot of

01:23:27.110 --> 01:23:30.170
our young guys that come on board.
Tim Ferriss: Got it. So they're not cut if

01:23:30.170 --> 01:23:33.400
they have a C in a particular —
Jake Muise: A C average.

01:23:33.400 --> 01:23:35.110
Tim Ferriss: — review point. It's the average.
Yeah. Got it.

01:23:35.110 --> 01:23:41.670
Jake Muise: So the C average, and it's been
amazing to see that system work. I've let

01:23:41.670 --> 01:23:48.130
go several people that were Cs that I would've
never let go — I wouldn't have known to

01:23:48.130 --> 01:23:52.949
let them go. I wouldn't have known that that
was the impact they were having on the team

01:23:52.949 --> 01:23:58.219
at large. It just would've never come out.
Tim Ferriss: And I would love to — you mentioned,

01:23:58.219 --> 01:24:01.340
I think you offered to maybe share the questions.
Jake Muise: Oh, absolutely.

01:24:01.340 --> 01:24:06.989
Tim Ferriss: So we'll put that in the show
notes as well, tim.blog/podcast, because I'm

01:24:06.989 --> 01:24:14.520
incredibly curious to check it out myself.
At least at face value, it seems like a very

01:24:14.520 --> 01:24:21.540
elegant solution to a lot of problems that
can seem like fragmented, separate problems

01:24:21.540 --> 01:24:26.650
you have to address in different ways.
Jake Muise: And again, I'm just a system builder,

01:24:26.650 --> 01:24:31.830
and by no means is it perfect, but I've heard
lots of people speak to how important these

01:24:31.830 --> 01:24:36.850
different personality traits are and how they
reward them. And more importantly than the

01:24:36.850 --> 01:24:44.020
C are the As. Being able to say to somebody,
"This incredible combination of humility,

01:24:44.020 --> 01:24:49.030
work ethic, and emotional intelligence is
making your whole team better." And your whole

01:24:49.030 --> 01:24:53.380
team is telling you you're extraordinary at
the thing. Being able to reward and compensate

01:24:53.380 --> 01:24:56.220
somebody for that and have a measure to do
so.

01:24:56.220 --> 01:24:59.820
Tim Ferriss: When you say compensate, so let's
say they have — I know this is getting in

01:24:59.820 --> 01:25:06.060
the weeds a bit, but I feel like that's where
a lot of the good stuff is hiding. So how

01:25:06.060 --> 01:25:10.710
many questions were there again?
Jake Muise: There's 18 questions. Six on humility,

01:25:10.710 --> 01:25:15.251
six on work ethic, and six on emotional intelligence.
Tim Ferriss: And they get this A, B, or C

01:25:15.251 --> 01:25:18.860
for each of those questions.
Jake Muise: Yeah. It's graded one through

01:25:18.860 --> 01:25:22.000
seven. We just did it because there was seven
days of the week, and we talk about being

01:25:22.000 --> 01:25:26.480
excellent every day. So it's one through seven.
And then we add of all the scores, which is

01:25:26.480 --> 01:25:31.090
126 total, and they get a percentage. So if
they're an 87, we give them a B-plus.

01:25:31.090 --> 01:25:32.710
Tim Ferriss: Which is the average or the total?
Jake Muise: Which is the average.

01:25:32.710 --> 01:25:36.500
Tim Ferriss: How do you reward or compensate
the As?

01:25:36.500 --> 01:25:42.489
Jake Muise: If they're in their first six
months of employment and they get two As or

01:25:42.489 --> 01:25:49.380
A-pluses in a reward, we give them a raise
based on that contribution. And then we celebrate

01:25:49.380 --> 01:25:54.630
with the team like "[inaudible name] was an
A this last quarter." And we make sure the

01:25:54.630 --> 01:25:59.011
team knows the contribution that they're having.
And what's so interesting, they already all

01:25:59.011 --> 01:26:00.300
know.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Right.

01:26:00.300 --> 01:26:07.340
Jake Muise: But to not have framework to reward
them for being amazing people has always been

01:26:07.340 --> 01:26:13.510
this fuzzy place for me where I couldn't reward
or compensate that person for being an extraordinary

01:26:13.510 --> 01:26:16.800
individual that was making their whole team
better because it didn't fit into what the

01:26:16.800 --> 01:26:21.850
classic hard skills define, as they're a great
shooter or they're a great driver or one of

01:26:21.850 --> 01:26:27.010
these different things. And every single one
of those A-plus people are our most highly

01:26:27.010 --> 01:26:32.070
skilled people as well. When you operate with
a certain level of humility, you are more

01:26:32.070 --> 01:26:37.080
willing to learn. And you learn faster.
And every single person that's come through

01:26:37.080 --> 01:26:43.090
our program that was a B-minus or a C that
was highly skilled had made the choice not

01:26:43.090 --> 01:26:50.980
to get better at these, what I very much consider
skills, just weeded themself out. It's been

01:26:50.980 --> 01:26:56.691
really cool to see people grow. And you're
in this camp and you see these guys. A lot

01:26:56.691 --> 01:27:03.030
of them will tape their score up above their
bed. And they'll like look at it in the morning

01:27:03.030 --> 01:27:07.969
and say, "Okay, my job is to make — I was
a B-minus on this thing. I need to bring more

01:27:07.969 --> 01:27:12.060
consistent energy every night." We've got
guys that go up and down and up and down and

01:27:12.060 --> 01:27:14.929
they're like, "Okay, I'm going to be trying
to be more consistent." They know what they're

01:27:14.929 --> 01:27:17.040
working on.
Tim Ferriss: That's so cool. This gave me

01:27:17.040 --> 01:27:21.659
a flashback, because when I was in high school,
and I wasn't particularly — just for whatever

01:27:21.659 --> 01:27:27.840
reason, I mean, I trained my ass off, but
I was not the most gifted wrestler. But I

01:27:27.840 --> 01:27:35.040
was pretty tough. And not 10 out of 10 tough,
but I ended up reading this book. It's, I'm

01:27:35.040 --> 01:27:39.230
sure, dated in a million ways, but it was
called Mental Toughness Training for Sports.

01:27:39.230 --> 01:27:43.150
And there was an assessment. And it was actually,
now that I think about it, pretty similar

01:27:43.150 --> 01:27:47.270
to HHS.
And I gave the assessment to five or six people

01:27:47.270 --> 01:27:55.050
and they did the assessment. And then I took
my score and that became my reminder. And

01:27:55.050 --> 01:28:00.110
that is when, in a single season, I mean,
I went from kind of middle of the pack to

01:28:00.110 --> 01:28:06.570
almost entirely undefeated until the very
end of the season. And it's not because I

01:28:06.570 --> 01:28:12.540
was the most gifted, but it's in part due
to having a constant reminder that is not

01:28:12.540 --> 01:28:17.860
from one person. You spot patterns and you're
like, "Okay, if it were just one person, I

01:28:17.860 --> 01:28:21.989
could maybe dismiss it. But now I'm getting
this from multiple people I respect." And

01:28:21.989 --> 01:28:25.563
it works, man. It really works.
Jake Muise: I think we're in a unique position

01:28:25.563 --> 01:28:26.563
where —
Tim Ferriss: And you could do this, I'm just

01:28:26.563 --> 01:28:32.880
thinking out loud, obviously. I was going
to say talking out loud, but that's a bit

01:28:32.880 --> 01:28:37.840
redundant. "I'm just talking out loud here,
folks." But you could use this outside of

01:28:37.840 --> 01:28:46.000
an employment context. You could take this
HHS and give it to five to 10 people you know

01:28:46.000 --> 01:28:50.920
and just ask them to be honest. That's the
prereq. I'm not asking you to be nice, I'm

01:28:50.920 --> 01:28:53.060
asking you to be accurate.
Jake Muise: And I want to give credit where

01:28:53.060 --> 01:28:58.580
it's due. Patrick Lencioni is an amazing — the
book The Ideal Team Player, which is what

01:28:58.580 --> 01:29:05.360
a lot of — the questions aren't based on
it, but this idea that — the original name,

01:29:05.360 --> 01:29:09.341
humble, hungry, smart, is from that book.
And so when somebody comes in, they have to

01:29:09.341 --> 01:29:13.179
read that book and it gives them the base
principles of what we're talking about. And

01:29:13.179 --> 01:29:20.400
then we basically design the questions. And
it's been incredible. You get young 25-year-olds

01:29:20.400 --> 01:29:24.489
that come in and their first couple of weeks
they're sitting in a meeting and they're hunched

01:29:24.489 --> 01:29:30.400
over and they got their hood on.
And typically, that's a hard conversation

01:29:30.400 --> 01:29:34.710
to have with somebody, to say, "I need you
to sit up, take your hood off, be an active

01:29:34.710 --> 01:29:39.830
listener." Because it's an uncomfortable thing
to talk to somebody about their body language.

01:29:39.830 --> 01:29:44.719
And you get to do that immediately because
the team has framework and language to say,

01:29:44.719 --> 01:29:48.320
"Hey, listen, brother. In three weeks you're
going to get graded. One of those things is

01:29:48.320 --> 01:29:52.239
body language. And if you just sit up in the
meeting, take your hood off, and make sure

01:29:52.239 --> 01:29:55.480
you are looking at the person talking, you're
going to get a better score. We like you,

01:29:55.480 --> 01:29:59.110
we want to keep you around." If you happen
to come in late —

01:29:59.110 --> 01:30:01.080
Tim Ferriss: It's set up front. It's not impromptu,
improvised.

01:30:01.080 --> 01:30:06.989
Jake Muise: It gives them the ability to have
hard conversations when somebody rolls in

01:30:06.989 --> 01:30:14.680
a little bit late, which is already unacceptable.
But if they don't genuinely apologize, somebody

01:30:14.680 --> 01:30:19.590
gets to come to them and say, "One of our
questions is about being genuine about your

01:30:19.590 --> 01:30:22.469
apologies if you've made mistakes. And we're
allowed to make mistakes here and we make

01:30:22.469 --> 01:30:27.250
a lot. But you come in and being like, 'Oh,
sorry guys.' Don't do that next time and it

01:30:27.250 --> 01:30:32.989
will be better." So it's outside of me, it's
given everybody else framework to have uncomfortable

01:30:32.989 --> 01:30:37.050
conversations and/or celebrate people.
Tim Ferriss: So The Ideal Team Player, Lencioni.

01:30:37.050 --> 01:30:38.190
This is assigned reading?
Jake Muise: Yes.

01:30:38.190 --> 01:30:40.730
Tim Ferriss: This is an assigned reading.
Do you have any other assigned reading?

01:30:40.730 --> 01:30:46.750
Jake Muise: Not for field teams. We have them
focus on that one for sure. For some of our

01:30:46.750 --> 01:30:53.060
other staff, we have Confessions of the Pricing
Man. Simon, I think is the last name.

01:30:53.060 --> 01:30:55.770
Tim Ferriss: I've read that book.
Jake Muise: It's really good. Anybody that

01:30:55.770 --> 01:30:58.199
deals with any type of product strategy, I
make sure they read that one.

01:30:58.199 --> 01:30:59.760
Tim Ferriss: I haven't thought of that book
in a long time.

01:30:59.760 --> 01:31:00.840
Jake Muise: Oh, it's so good.
Tim Ferriss: Confessions of the Pricing Man.

01:31:00.840 --> 01:31:06.830
Jake Muise: And then The Road Less Stupid.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. I don't know this one.

01:31:06.830 --> 01:31:10.340
Jake Muise: It is one of my favorite general
business books. And I'm drawing a blank on

01:31:10.340 --> 01:31:11.869
the —
Tim Ferriss: It's okay. It's memorable.

01:31:11.869 --> 01:31:16.010
Jake Muise: I've read a lot, actually. Chris
Ashenden recommended it to me.

01:31:16.010 --> 01:31:19.150
Tim Ferriss: For those who don't know, Chris
Ashenden is the founder of AG1. Previously

01:31:19.150 --> 01:31:22.110
Athletic Greens.
Jake Muise: Yeah. And he's been an amazing

01:31:22.110 --> 01:31:24.210
guy whose always handed me homework. He's
an amazing —

01:31:24.210 --> 01:31:29.770
Tim Ferriss: He's a great operator.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So again, I'm happy to share

01:31:29.770 --> 01:31:33.020
all of the questions. And I think when people
see the questions, actually, they're tailored

01:31:33.020 --> 01:31:38.130
to our teams, but it's actually pretty easy
to tailor those questions to your specific

01:31:38.130 --> 01:31:44.960
work format. I haven't quite found a way to
move them into the virtual Zoom world because

01:31:44.960 --> 01:31:49.270
a lot of them are so tactile in how people
work together in workflows. And there will

01:31:49.270 --> 01:31:53.880
be several iterations, we just changed it
again. But in its current format and framework,

01:31:53.880 --> 01:31:55.750
I think it could be valuable to people. I'm
happy to share that.

01:31:55.750 --> 01:31:59.610
Tim Ferriss: Amazing. Yeah. We'll put it in
the show notes for folks to check out. I promised

01:31:59.610 --> 01:32:05.460
it earlier, so I want to not be remiss and
come back to it as this conversational boomerang

01:32:05.460 --> 01:32:08.620
returns to me. Nutrient density.
Jake Muise: Got it.

01:32:08.620 --> 01:32:13.679
Tim Ferriss: How should we tackle this? Where
would you like to start?

01:32:13.679 --> 01:32:17.490
Jake Muise: We could start at when —
Tim Ferriss: This is true for plants.

01:32:17.490 --> 01:32:19.660
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Also true for meat. Anything

01:32:19.660 --> 01:32:25.360
you put in your mouth has a certain composition.
And just because it's called the same thing,

01:32:25.360 --> 01:32:30.320
banana, banana, banana, green bean, green
bean, green bean, does not mean they are equal

01:32:30.320 --> 01:32:33.040
in nutritional value.
Jake Muise: I think, and I'll start at maybe

01:32:33.040 --> 01:32:42.619
the end, I think nutrient density, i.e., food
quality, and being able to measure that accurately

01:32:42.619 --> 01:32:47.400
and someone be able to understand it in its
simplest form, i.e., on a label, is going

01:32:47.400 --> 01:32:53.170
to completely change our food system and be
the lever for regenerative agriculture.

01:32:53.170 --> 01:32:58.330
Tim Ferriss: Meaning having an indicator on
a label, some type of measurement. Just like

01:32:58.330 --> 01:33:00.005
you look at calories, just like you look at
fill in the blank.

01:33:00.005 --> 01:33:03.280
Jake Muise: If you walked into a store tomorrow
and you flipped it over and there was two

01:33:03.280 --> 01:33:08.980
blueberries and one said 92 and one said 76,
and you knew that 92 meant that was that much

01:33:08.980 --> 01:33:15.250
better for you, measuring thousands of biochemicals
that they now can through metabolomic testing,

01:33:15.250 --> 01:33:20.830
you're going to buy the one. And what's so
amazing about the conversation of nutrient

01:33:20.830 --> 01:33:26.850
density is that is nutrition of place. And
when you think of regenerative agriculture

01:33:26.850 --> 01:33:31.110
practices, which is basically just layering
conservation practices, like we're taking

01:33:31.110 --> 01:33:33.950
care of soil, we're taking care of water,
we're doing these different things.

01:33:33.950 --> 01:33:37.010
Tim Ferriss: Which people have done for a
long time in certain cultures.

01:33:37.010 --> 01:33:41.880
Jake Muise: And we got away for for a long
time and now we're coming back to it. And

01:33:41.880 --> 01:33:47.960
if you knew the quality of your food, not
measured by the five metrics that are currently

01:33:47.960 --> 01:33:52.590
available, but measured by the thousands of
biochemicals that we can now test for, both

01:33:52.590 --> 01:33:59.100
good and bad. And there's billions of dollars
going into this. The Patagonia Provisions

01:33:59.100 --> 01:34:04.480
were like, "This is coming for sure." And
what was really interesting for us is we got

01:34:04.480 --> 01:34:12.670
to be one of the first people to be fully
tested. So about two years ago, we submitted

01:34:12.670 --> 01:34:20.770
our bone broth to a typical USDA FSNS lab
to make our label. And I remember them, they

01:34:20.770 --> 01:34:26.430
called me and they said, "Something's wrong
with these bones." And I was like, "We sent

01:34:26.430 --> 01:34:28.659
you the bone broth that were made from regular
bones."

01:34:28.659 --> 01:34:34.510
They had thought we had put some form of protein
powder or additional collagen in there because

01:34:34.510 --> 01:34:39.690
it tested 33 percent higher in protein per
ounce than anything they'd ever seen. So they

01:34:39.690 --> 01:34:45.640
had to retest it and it came out the exact
same way. And our mission was never nutrient

01:34:45.640 --> 01:34:49.889
density, our mission was to balance populations.
And I always knew it was better for me because

01:34:49.889 --> 01:34:54.120
that animal had a choice to eat. But when
we got that testing, we were like, "Wait a

01:34:54.120 --> 01:34:57.730
second, what is going on here?" So we sent
more, we sent livers and hearts and different

01:34:57.730 --> 01:35:02.770
cuts into a conventional lab for testing.
Sure enough, lab emails me in the middle of

01:35:02.770 --> 01:35:06.880
it. I remember this one. They're like, "There's
something wrong with the hearts. They have

01:35:06.880 --> 01:35:12.171
too much choline." I'm like, "There's nothing
wrong. We just sent you a heart" And we were

01:35:12.171 --> 01:35:19.070
working with — did you introduce us to Anthony
Gustin? Who? We got to Anthony Gustin?

01:35:19.070 --> 01:35:22.520
Tim Ferriss: I don't think so, but I do know.
I know who he is. Absolutely.

01:35:22.520 --> 01:35:23.540
Jake Muise: Okay. So we were working with
—

01:35:23.540 --> 01:35:27.230
Tim Ferriss: He's helped other people I know.
Jake Muise: We were trying to figure out and

01:35:27.230 --> 01:35:33.230
compare after we got that information to a
typical comparison to beef. What he pointed

01:35:33.230 --> 01:35:39.370
us to, which was crazy and scary, is the vast
majority of the USDA databases from the 1930s.

01:35:39.370 --> 01:35:42.070
Tim Ferriss: I wonder if I made that intro.
It's possible.

01:35:42.070 --> 01:35:43.790
Jake Muise: You've made a lot of them, my
friend.

01:35:43.790 --> 01:35:46.840
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's possible.
Jake Muise: I appreciate it. But what we found

01:35:46.840 --> 01:35:52.960
out is that the nutritional comparison to
beef weren't even actually able to do it because

01:35:52.960 --> 01:35:59.140
the data was so dated and you're able to be
off within 20 percent on a label, which is

01:35:59.140 --> 01:36:01.369
a whole other story.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. People who are packaging

01:36:01.369 --> 01:36:03.900
things have more wiggle room than a lot of
consumers realize.

01:36:03.900 --> 01:36:06.290
Jake Muise: 20 percent wiggle room.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's wild.

01:36:06.290 --> 01:36:08.770
Jake Muise: Up or down, whichever one favors
them.

01:36:08.770 --> 01:36:11.850
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jake Muise: Yeah. So Dr. Gustin introduced

01:36:11.850 --> 01:36:17.950
us to somebody, Dr. van Vliet at Utah State
University, who has probably one of the most

01:36:17.950 --> 01:36:24.060
advanced food labs in North America. And he
was doing the largest beef study ever done,

01:36:24.060 --> 01:36:30.639
and the most in-depth, measuring a thousand-plus
biochemicals through disadvanced metabolomic

01:36:30.639 --> 01:36:36.860
testing. We just happened to get tossed in
at the end of it. So there's 200-plus beef

01:36:36.860 --> 01:36:39.050
—
Tim Ferriss: Little spritz of axis deer.

01:36:39.050 --> 01:36:43.480
Jake Muise: There's 200-plus beef samples.
And then they threw us at the end and he thought

01:36:43.480 --> 01:36:49.000
like, "Oh, this will be really cool to see
an animal that has a choice to what it eats."

01:36:49.000 --> 01:36:57.600
What that might look like in what we understood
as a fairly fertile area, right? Okay. Comes

01:36:57.600 --> 01:37:03.690
out, two to four X the phytochemicals, so
all of the good stuff from plants that gets

01:37:03.690 --> 01:37:11.020
transferred into meat were two to four X.
Were eight to 64 X the omega-3s, so like DHA

01:37:11.020 --> 01:37:16.119
was 20 times — it was just, every single
category was absurd. And then probably the

01:37:16.119 --> 01:37:21.400
most exciting for me is they measured oxidative
stress. The stress in animal goes through

01:37:21.400 --> 01:37:29.750
during that process, you can measure that
oxidative stress and its impact on — there's

01:37:29.750 --> 01:37:33.140
a whole bunch of chemicals I still don't understand.
I'm not a nutritionist, but they were able

01:37:33.140 --> 01:37:38.710
to measure the negative impacts of stress.
And of course ours was essentially zero and

01:37:38.710 --> 01:37:43.690
the cattle, the beef stuff, was all over the
place. It was pretty scary. But what was most,

01:37:43.690 --> 01:37:50.560
when I saw it, the most exciting thing, honestly,
wasn't Maui Nui. It was, A, this is nutrition

01:37:50.560 --> 01:37:58.020
of place. This is a direct reflection of how
well and how fertile a place is because all

01:37:58.020 --> 01:38:02.790
of that nutrient density is coming from the
plants that they're eating in, i.e., the soil

01:38:02.790 --> 01:38:04.340
—
Tim Ferriss: The soil, exactly. And the water.

01:38:04.340 --> 01:38:10.080
Jake Muise: Yeah. And the water moving through
the soil and instantly realized if every consumer

01:38:10.080 --> 01:38:18.860
had this level of insight into the nutritional
quality of their food, it'll completely change

01:38:18.860 --> 01:38:20.770
the way people look at that food system. And
then we got to —

01:38:20.770 --> 01:38:25.150
Tim Ferriss: And then it'll change the entire
sort of supply chain production side.

01:38:25.150 --> 01:38:28.270
Jake Muise: Well, it's not a race to the bottom
anymore of producing commodities, which is

01:38:28.270 --> 01:38:34.449
the most you can at the cheapest price. I
mean, this is my favorite saying. Every time

01:38:34.449 --> 01:38:39.820
Peter Attia says we're over-nourished, I'm
just like, "Yes." We're producing an absurd

01:38:39.820 --> 01:38:46.310
amount of calories that aren't good for us.
It's coming and we're going to understand

01:38:46.310 --> 01:38:50.750
the nutritional quality of our food and it
will be graded and easy to know. And that

01:38:50.750 --> 01:38:57.340
will create these direct relationships with
these, what will probably be regenerative

01:38:57.340 --> 01:39:02.290
agri-practices that are fostering the health
of these places. Then so when we dug even

01:39:02.290 --> 01:39:08.080
further, we found out that the leeward slopes
of volcanic —

01:39:08.080 --> 01:39:10.880
Tim Ferriss: What does leeward mean?
Jake Muise: It is the windward side of a slope.

01:39:10.880 --> 01:39:13.750
Tim Ferriss: Got it.
Jake Muise: Yeah. Hawai’i Haleakalā has

01:39:13.750 --> 01:39:20.179
a very particular soil called andisols, and
then there are three types of andisols. Andisols

01:39:20.179 --> 01:39:26.610
are some of the most fertile soils in the
world. They hold water better and hold more

01:39:26.610 --> 01:39:32.620
nutrients from those waters better. And then
of the three andisol soil types, we have one

01:39:32.620 --> 01:39:39.640
called ustand, and it's the most fertile of
the three. So, the slopes we're harvesting

01:39:39.640 --> 01:39:47.699
these deer on are actually, I think the ustands
make up 0.05 percent of the world's soils.

01:39:47.699 --> 01:39:50.230
Tim Ferriss: That's wild.
Jake Muise: We happened to be —

01:39:50.230 --> 01:39:54.040
Tim Ferriss: Luck of the draw.
Jake Muise: Yeah. And that's the thing. Again,

01:39:54.040 --> 01:39:58.010
this was never our mission. The mission was
just to balance populations, but we happened

01:39:58.010 --> 01:40:03.860
to have one of the most virile animals in
the world that has a choice to eat exactly

01:40:03.860 --> 01:40:10.159
what it wants, moving through the most fertile
slopes maybe on the planet. I think that's

01:40:10.159 --> 01:40:16.250
what Dr. van Vliet got so excited about is
he now has a marker to say when there's an

01:40:16.250 --> 01:40:21.190
extremely fertile place that's like for most
part well taken care of in a lot of the areas

01:40:21.190 --> 01:40:24.770
because they're able to go to the very best
areas, this is what nutrient density could

01:40:24.770 --> 01:40:33.710
look like. And it's, vitamin A was 800 times
in the liver versus some of these beef livers.

01:40:33.710 --> 01:40:41.530
Tim Ferriss: To come back to one point, the
DHA, I don't know if there's EPA in axis deer.

01:40:41.530 --> 01:40:43.810
I have no idea. I'm not a lipidologist, if
that's the right term.

01:40:43.810 --> 01:40:47.690
Jake Muise: I think there is, yeah.
Tim Ferriss: On the omega-3, side is eating

01:40:47.690 --> 01:40:52.950
axis deer in terms of levels of omega-3s,
I guess we'd have to think about the sort

01:40:52.950 --> 01:41:00.880
of concentration per ounce, but is it comparable
to eating some types of fish or would it still

01:41:00.880 --> 01:41:03.369
be significantly lower than fish?
Jake Muise: Well, we don't know the answer

01:41:03.369 --> 01:41:07.280
to that yet because the study measured it
in comparison to beef.

01:41:07.280 --> 01:41:11.560
Tim Ferriss: Oh, right. I see. So the percentages
and so on were benchmarked to beef.

01:41:11.560 --> 01:41:15.030
Jake Muise: The new piece of equipment that
Dr. van Vliet has coming in will be able to

01:41:15.030 --> 01:41:20.790
give us measures in milligrams, micrograms.
We'll know that in the near future here, and

01:41:20.790 --> 01:41:25.760
this is what ultimately, there's several companies
working on this. Audacious is one of them,

01:41:25.760 --> 01:41:30.739
this amazing company. They're going to be
able to measure these thousands of biochemicals

01:41:30.739 --> 01:41:36.110
to the form that you need to turn them into
a label. But we know, to answer your question,

01:41:36.110 --> 01:41:40.600
we know even in comparison to beef, it is
about halfway to salmon. So it's actually

01:41:40.600 --> 01:41:48.151
a significant source of omega-3s that, for
some people that don't eat fish, it goes a

01:41:48.151 --> 01:41:53.409
long way. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Little inside joke, because

01:41:53.409 --> 01:41:58.220
before we pressed record, we were talking
about some very, very well known professional

01:41:58.220 --> 01:42:04.210
sports teams who are using Maui Nui to feed
their players. And some of these players,

01:42:04.210 --> 01:42:07.870
for whatever reason, just won't eat fish,
but they will eat Maui Nui.

01:42:07.870 --> 01:42:13.639
Jake Muise: Well, and blood test every week,
these extraordinary athletes. And we've just

01:42:13.639 --> 01:42:16.570
started working with a lot of these sports
nutritionists, and they're going to be able

01:42:16.570 --> 01:42:20.180
to measure for us if it's having an impact
on these athletes.

01:42:20.180 --> 01:42:21.400
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's cool. That's so
cool.

01:42:21.400 --> 01:42:25.790
Jake Muise: Just really, again, never part
of the mission for us, but an understanding

01:42:25.790 --> 01:42:31.520
of place. And this is what gets cool. My wife's
so excited and she's like, this comes back

01:42:31.520 --> 01:42:40.889
to that conversation of innovation is a culture.
Hawaiians were able to produce extraordinary

01:42:40.889 --> 01:42:47.270
nutrient rich foods with finite resources
for a million people. And Hawai’i now imports

01:42:47.270 --> 01:42:55.620
95 percent of its food and half of it's Twinkie-level
nutritional value. It really points back to,

01:42:55.620 --> 01:43:00.150
I think our place is informing what we're
doing. And it's really fun to have such a

01:43:00.150 --> 01:43:04.830
strong connection to place and it helped to
inform your solution.

01:43:04.830 --> 01:43:09.610
Tim Ferriss: Connection to place, you have
Kuʻulani, you have the little ones. Oh, yeah.

01:43:09.610 --> 01:43:11.770
Jake Muise: Right?
Tim Ferriss: You live in Hawai’i. Let's

01:43:11.770 --> 01:43:15.850
talk about the family for a second. Uh, oh.
Clawback allowances.

01:43:15.850 --> 01:43:18.091
Jake Muise: Man, I get to give you credit
for this.

01:43:18.091 --> 01:43:19.750
Tim Ferriss: This is —
Jake Muise: Wait till you hear this one.

01:43:19.750 --> 01:43:22.489
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Okay. Fire away.
Jake Muise: John List.

01:43:22.489 --> 01:43:24.550
Tim Ferriss: I was just thinking about John
List.

01:43:24.550 --> 01:43:28.360
Jake Muise: John List, the economist you had
on the show, and he was talking about using

01:43:28.360 --> 01:43:32.810
clawback allowances in a professional format,
but I remember him talking about using it

01:43:32.810 --> 01:43:39.010
to potty-train his kids. And this idea of
loss aversion, like I already have it and

01:43:39.010 --> 01:43:42.150
you're going to take it away from me being
much stronger than the opposite.

01:43:42.150 --> 01:43:46.300
Tim Ferriss: Right. How hard will you work
to make $50 versus how hard will you work

01:43:46.300 --> 01:43:48.840
if you feel like $50 has been taken away from
you?

01:43:48.840 --> 01:43:50.940
Jake Muise: Exactly.
Tim Ferriss: The latter.

01:43:50.940 --> 01:43:54.250
Jake Muise: We had been struggling with —
Tim Ferriss: Equals a lot more.

01:43:54.250 --> 01:44:00.560
Jake Muise: — allowances for the kids. And
we make lots of mistakes as parents. And what

01:44:00.560 --> 01:44:05.290
the allowances were accidentally doing were
they were training them — they had jobs

01:44:05.290 --> 01:44:11.900
and they did their jobs and then they earned
their money if they did their jobs. But what

01:44:11.900 --> 01:44:15.222
it was doing is when we asked them to do extra
stuff and be helpful, it was like, "Well,

01:44:15.222 --> 01:44:23.090
am I going to make more money?" And we had
taught them this system that was the opposite

01:44:23.090 --> 01:44:26.330
of what we were trying to teach them, which
was to try and be helpful. And me and Ku'ula,

01:44:26.330 --> 01:44:32.409
I remember her listening to it six hours later
than I did. And she's like, "Did you hear

01:44:32.409 --> 01:44:33.610
about the clawback allowances? Did you hear
about clawback?"

01:44:33.610 --> 01:44:37.349
I was like, "Yes." And we both thought of
it instantly. "Let's do it for allowances."

01:44:37.349 --> 01:44:43.489
And so what we do now, and we changed two
things. We put $20 in ones on the fridge at

01:44:43.489 --> 01:44:50.020
the beginning of the month and we say, "This
is yours. And what we do now is we claw back

01:44:50.020 --> 01:44:55.929
those dollar bills for attitude instead of
jobs." What we do is when we say, "Palikū,

01:44:55.929 --> 01:45:00.830
can you go feed the dogs?" And he's like,
"Ugh." We just walk to the fridge and we pull

01:45:00.830 --> 01:45:07.530
off a dollar and it was this amazing exercise
over a period of three months to, it was straight

01:45:07.530 --> 01:45:10.580
revolt, "What are you talking about?"
Tim Ferriss: Mutiny on the ship.

01:45:10.580 --> 01:45:15.489
Jake Muise: And revolt turned into a little
bit of sarcasm. Like, "Yeah, fine. I'll do

01:45:15.489 --> 01:45:20.800
that." Because they didn't want to lose their
money. One step back. We don't buy them anything.

01:45:20.800 --> 01:45:24.150
We're kind of strict when it comes to, they
get stuff at birthdays and they get stuff

01:45:24.150 --> 01:45:28.159
at Christmas, but other than that, they need
to be useful people and earn their money.

01:45:28.159 --> 01:45:34.050
And so they really value the dollars they
get at the end of the month. And it went from

01:45:34.050 --> 01:45:42.430
mutiny to sarcasm. I remember it happening,
and I remember asking Leiʻohu to grab the

01:45:42.430 --> 01:45:47.869
laundry, and she's like, "Sure." And I was
like, "Wait a second." And I looked at Ku'ula,

01:45:47.869 --> 01:45:51.369
I was like, "That actually just worked." Don't
get me wrong, don't get me wrong. They're

01:45:51.369 --> 01:45:52.369
amazing kids. They're 99 percent of the time
—

01:45:52.369 --> 01:45:54.340
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, but they're kids, they're
little humans.

01:45:54.340 --> 01:45:59.420
Jake Muise: And now, just a couple weeks ago
I came home and Palikū was like, "Oh, I cleaned

01:45:59.420 --> 01:46:06.190
the car." And I was like, "What? What's wrong
with you?" It was this odd thing that a combination

01:46:06.190 --> 01:46:14.090
of the clawback allowance and training for
attitude instead of them earning for a task.

01:46:14.090 --> 01:46:17.040
If it helps anybody, again, we're not amazing
parents by any means, but —

01:46:17.040 --> 01:46:20.400
Tim Ferriss: I think you're pretty good parents,
from what I can tell.

01:46:20.400 --> 01:46:23.889
Jake Muise: That small trick, oh, boy. It's
been a big one for us for sure.

01:46:23.889 --> 01:46:27.409
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to toggle around a
little bit here. I have some notes in front

01:46:27.409 --> 01:46:30.180
of me. We're talking about the kids.
Jake Muise: Yep.

01:46:30.180 --> 01:46:34.489
Tim Ferriss: Secret Pinterest boards.
Jake Muise: Oh, man. This is another one.

01:46:34.489 --> 01:46:38.270
Tim Ferriss: For the relationship between
you and your wife.

01:46:38.270 --> 01:46:39.820
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: All right. I know you're not

01:46:39.820 --> 01:46:41.980
allowed to talk about it, but it's just me
and you.

01:46:41.980 --> 01:46:44.400
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Just us sitting at this table.

01:46:44.400 --> 01:46:46.501
Jake Muise: Well, I've actually —
Tim Ferriss: Not millions of people.

01:46:46.501 --> 01:46:50.530
Jake Muise: I've actually tried to talk about
this with a couple of our friends, and this

01:46:50.530 --> 01:46:53.810
is Fight Club level, "You don't talk about
this."

01:46:53.810 --> 01:46:56.869
Tim Ferriss: Exclusive here first, folks,
Tim Ferriss Show.

01:46:56.869 --> 01:47:00.880
Jake Muise: All of the credit, much of my
credit goes to my amazing wife, but all of

01:47:00.880 --> 01:47:04.190
the credit for this one goes to her. And there's
like, this has to happen in a very, you have

01:47:04.190 --> 01:47:08.380
to follow the rules of this one or it doesn't
work. Early on in our relationship, I think

01:47:08.380 --> 01:47:14.310
I'm two years in, she's an extraordinary person,
have no idea what to buy for her. I've failed

01:47:14.310 --> 01:47:18.790
several, several times, and she's not like,
this is not — she barely will spend a hundred

01:47:18.790 --> 01:47:22.760
dollars on herself a couple times a year.
She's that type of person. She thrifts 99

01:47:22.760 --> 01:47:26.860
percent of her clothes, et cetera, et cetera.
So it's just the worst person ever to try

01:47:26.860 --> 01:47:32.020
and buy stuff for. She's so picky.
And she sits me down, I give her a birthday

01:47:32.020 --> 01:47:36.710
gift, I get it all wrong. She sits me down
the next day and she's like, "This is what

01:47:36.710 --> 01:47:41.480
we're going to do. A, we're never going to
talk about this conversation again. And B,

01:47:41.480 --> 01:47:47.310
I've created a secret Pinterest board that
I've invited you to. Every item on there is

01:47:47.310 --> 01:47:53.320
something that I want. It's exactly what I
want. The link goes exactly to it. But here's

01:47:53.320 --> 01:47:58.440
how this works. You are never allowed to talk
about this, and you're never allowed to give

01:47:58.440 --> 01:48:01.840
it to me for a birthday or something." And
we're not big on birthdays anyways. And she's

01:48:01.840 --> 01:48:08.270
like, "I will pretend that you did it in every
single instance." No, this is the most important

01:48:08.270 --> 01:48:09.270
part, okay?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Right.

01:48:09.270 --> 01:48:15.100
Jake Muise: My children think I'm a genius.
They'll come home and they'll just be like

01:48:15.100 --> 01:48:20.810
this random box on the table that has these
beautiful earrings in them and Kuʻula will

01:48:20.810 --> 01:48:27.969
come in and she'll just be like, "Oh, my God.
How did you know? This is exactly what I wanted.

01:48:27.969 --> 01:48:33.230
I can't believe you found them. I've been
looking for them forever." And my girls will

01:48:33.230 --> 01:48:38.730
look at me like, "That's amazing, dad. How
did you know?" She does it so well, half the

01:48:38.730 --> 01:48:45.990
time I think, "Oh, my God. I'm amazing." She
has fake cried several times. She does it

01:48:45.990 --> 01:48:51.180
in front of her mom.
The only way this works is I believe half

01:48:51.180 --> 01:48:56.190
the time that I've done this amazing thing
for her. That's how important it is. And it

01:48:56.190 --> 01:49:03.920
has been the greatest. I never ever think
about buying her anything. Anytime I even

01:49:03.920 --> 01:49:07.670
have the slightest inclination, I go straight
to the Pinterest board and I just grab one

01:49:07.670 --> 01:49:14.560
thing off of it and it shows up. And she does
this amazing job of, "It's the biggest deal.

01:49:14.560 --> 01:49:18.630
Your dad's the most amazing person for be
able to figure this thing out." And they all

01:49:18.630 --> 01:49:20.790
believe it. And we're never allowed to tell
anybody about it.

01:49:20.790 --> 01:49:24.460
Tim Ferriss: What a great secret.
Jake Muise: Oh, it is so — she's done it

01:49:24.460 --> 01:49:29.449
in front of couples and the guys are just,
"How the hell, how did you know?" I'm like,

01:49:29.449 --> 01:49:35.590
"I just pay attention, man. I just figure
it out." She just makes me look so good. And

01:49:35.590 --> 01:49:41.710
she gets exactly what she wants. I tried to
bring it up once when her mom was around and

01:49:41.710 --> 01:49:44.250
she looked at me like she was going to kill
me. She's just like, "We don't talk about

01:49:44.250 --> 01:49:46.219
it or else it goes away and it doesn't work."
Tim Ferriss: You know the rules.

01:49:46.219 --> 01:49:47.679
Jake Muise: Oh, man.
Tim Ferriss: Bite your tongue.

01:49:47.679 --> 01:49:53.160
Jake Muise: It's so good. It is. She has to
pretend it's real, and I feel amazing.

01:49:53.160 --> 01:50:00.159
Tim Ferriss: Win, win. Oh, win, win, win,
win, win, win, on so many levels. All right.

01:50:00.159 --> 01:50:02.610
I'm going to let you choose where we go next.
Jake Muise: Okay.

01:50:02.610 --> 01:50:08.070
Tim Ferriss: Old lady crying on the couch
game, or tug of war?

01:50:08.070 --> 01:50:14.130
Jake Muise: Okay. I'm going to stick with
my wife's a genius one here, and we'll give

01:50:14.130 --> 01:50:18.380
her the old lady on the couch game. She, for
years — and I didn't ask about it until

01:50:18.380 --> 01:50:26.389
recently. She, for years, has done this thing
and I didn't know what it was. And when she

01:50:26.389 --> 01:50:30.960
was frustrated or something was going on,
she would close her eyes and instantly turn

01:50:30.960 --> 01:50:34.440
into the happiest person in the world and
be loving our kids, even if they were monsters.

01:50:34.440 --> 01:50:38.489
And I didn't know what was happening. And
so I finally asked her, I was like, "Okay,

01:50:38.489 --> 01:50:41.880
what is that thing you're doing?"
And she's like, "That's my old lady crying

01:50:41.880 --> 01:50:48.130
on the couch game." And I'm like, "What is
that?" And she said, "When I'm feeling frustrated

01:50:48.130 --> 01:50:56.580
with my kids, I pretend I'm 80 years old."
And she's amazing. She really closes her eyes

01:50:56.580 --> 01:51:01.310
and she pretends she's 80 years old and she
says, "I'm given the opportunity, it's my

01:51:01.310 --> 01:51:05.210
birthday, or it's something, and I'm given
the opportunity to use a time machine, but

01:51:05.210 --> 01:51:12.290
I am only allowed to go back to this exact
moment." And she really does it. She closes

01:51:12.290 --> 01:51:18.000
her eyes and she opens her eyes and she's
80 and she has five minutes to be this 80-year-old

01:51:18.000 --> 01:51:24.860
lady looking at her nine-year-old daughter
again after she's been grown up. And she can

01:51:24.860 --> 01:51:29.650
open her eyes and she just stands up and she's
like, "Oh, she's so cute." And she kisses

01:51:29.650 --> 01:51:33.300
their face and the kids know what the game
is. And they're just like, "Oh, Mom. You're

01:51:33.300 --> 01:51:38.210
doing that thing again."
But she genuinely can put herself in the place

01:51:38.210 --> 01:51:44.989
of gratitude to be back in that moment, seeing
her kids again after 30 or 40 years of them

01:51:44.989 --> 01:51:50.760
being grown and old. And it's just watching
her do it is amazing. And then I have tried

01:51:50.760 --> 01:51:54.790
it several times since she told me. If you
really put yourself in that situation, I've

01:51:54.790 --> 01:52:00.630
done it with my son. He's turning 14 and he's
got hair under his arm. It's happening, puberty's

01:52:00.630 --> 01:52:06.410
happening, it's happening. And I only have
four more years with him until he graduates.

01:52:06.410 --> 01:52:11.250
And a couple times I found myself frustrated
with him. I close my eyes and I think when

01:52:11.250 --> 01:52:15.370
I'm 80 and my knees are sore, what would I
feel to be able to go back and see him at

01:52:15.370 --> 01:52:19.619
14 when he's in this place?
Tim Ferriss: Your self-care at 80 is amazing.

01:52:19.619 --> 01:52:22.489
If you're just like, "When I'm 80 and my knees
start hurting," but yeah, I get it.

01:52:22.489 --> 01:52:25.889
Jake Muise: Well, no. I've got big plans,
and I've got Peter Attia as my friend. But

01:52:25.889 --> 01:52:31.209
it's this, she's a genius. In some of the
most frustrating moments where you've like

01:52:31.209 --> 01:52:35.270
— kids are your greatest joy and they will
frustrate you more than anybody on the planet

01:52:35.270 --> 01:52:42.670
can. And she's just found this way to have
gratitude for that time because it's gone.

01:52:42.670 --> 01:52:49.179
And she calls it old lady crying on the couch
game because she cries. She'll be tearing

01:52:49.179 --> 01:52:52.000
up and the kids will be like, "Oh, God. Here
we go again." Because they get kissed all

01:52:52.000 --> 01:52:57.870
over the face and, "Oh, you're so small and
cute and it's amazing." And yeah, she's amazing

01:52:57.870 --> 01:53:02.329
at finding ways to be grateful for sure.
Tim Ferriss: This, I'm really grateful you

01:53:02.329 --> 01:53:07.570
brought this up 'cause it reminded me of something
that I found super valuable for a couple of

01:53:07.570 --> 01:53:12.290
years, and then as often happens, I just kind
of forgot about it, which was for Tribe of

01:53:12.290 --> 01:53:16.710
Mentors, so my last book, there were many
people featured and one of the questions I

01:53:16.710 --> 01:53:21.840
asked almost everybody was, "When you were
feeling overwhelmed or frustrated, what do

01:53:21.840 --> 01:53:27.800
you do?"
And one of the answers was, "When I'm feeling

01:53:27.800 --> 01:53:34.699
overwhelmed or frustrated, I look at whatever
this mundane situation is," and I'll give

01:53:34.699 --> 01:53:39.920
a personal example. If I'm laying in a hammock,
and not necessarily — I could be frustrated

01:53:39.920 --> 01:53:43.210
or preoccupied by something, but my dog Molly's
playing with a stick and I'm just kind of

01:53:43.210 --> 01:53:49.400
chilling. I ask myself, "50 years from now,
how much would I pay to come back to this

01:53:49.400 --> 01:53:54.820
moment for just 10 minutes?" Similar trick,
right?

01:53:54.820 --> 01:53:56.929
Jake Muise: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: But I haven't used it.

01:53:56.929 --> 01:53:59.750
Jake Muise: It's so good, man.
Tim Ferriss: And this is the perfect reboot.

01:53:59.750 --> 01:54:03.030
So, thank you, Ku'u.
Jake Muise: Oh, yeah. Again, she gets the

01:54:03.030 --> 01:54:06.920
vast majority of the credit for anything that's
smart that comes out of my mouth.

01:54:06.920 --> 01:54:11.770
Tim Ferriss: Tug of war.
Jake Muise: Okay, this one, Ric Elias, founder

01:54:11.770 --> 01:54:15.960
of Red Ventures, introduced to me through
Peter gets all of the credit, but it was so

01:54:15.960 --> 01:54:19.639
good. I just, it's something that I started
doing that I have to share. And he described

01:54:19.639 --> 01:54:25.329
— this is the last parenting one, I promise.
He described parenting as a tug of war you

01:54:25.329 --> 01:54:27.739
have to lose.
Tim Ferriss: Okay?

01:54:27.739 --> 01:54:33.440
Jake Muise: It's really interesting when you
have kids and you hold onto them. I've heard

01:54:33.440 --> 01:54:36.650
lots of different parenting advice, and the
reason it stuck with me so well is it's such

01:54:36.650 --> 01:54:42.500
a great visual of your kids pulling a line
across from you and that line moving through

01:54:42.500 --> 01:54:47.209
your hands. When they're two and three years
old, you don't even have to hold it. You're

01:54:47.209 --> 01:54:51.610
just like, "Whatever. You can't pull that
thing." And then they get six or seven and

01:54:51.610 --> 01:54:55.640
they give it a little tug and you're like,
"Oh, well, I've got to hold onto this thing."

01:54:55.640 --> 01:55:01.380
And then the best part about that advice is
knowing you have to lose. So knowing when

01:55:01.380 --> 01:55:07.020
they're 18 or 19, you have to have fully let
go of that rope. The visual that comes to

01:55:07.020 --> 01:55:14.170
my mind all the time is just this really slow,
steady release of that rope instead of these

01:55:14.170 --> 01:55:20.670
giant jumps or these parenting mistakes that
you're not letting them become independent

01:55:20.670 --> 01:55:26.650
and responsible and useful. You're trying
to teach them all of these things so that

01:55:26.650 --> 01:55:33.100
you can let go of the rope at the end of it,
and they're okay. You're trying to spare them

01:55:33.100 --> 01:55:38.340
the feeling of being useless.
Tim Ferriss: How does that impact that metaphor

01:55:38.340 --> 01:55:43.579
or analogy? How did that affect how you parent
or make decisions?

01:55:43.579 --> 01:55:48.130
Jake Muise: Good example. Leiʻohu asked for
her first sleepover, which we've been pretty

01:55:48.130 --> 01:55:54.711
stringent about because she wants to go an
hour away and sleep in a house. My immediate

01:55:54.711 --> 01:56:01.900
reaction was, "No." And then the thought in
my head was like, "Wait a second, she's 12,

01:56:01.900 --> 01:56:06.449
and if you're going to let go of the rope
a little bit, you're going to have to let

01:56:06.449 --> 01:56:10.421
it all the way go. This is probably the right
situation to let it go a little bit." Make

01:56:10.421 --> 01:56:14.650
sure it's safe and you call the parents and
you figure out what it is. But it reminds

01:56:14.650 --> 01:56:19.790
me, the best actionable use of it is knowing
you have to let it go, knowing it has to be

01:56:19.790 --> 01:56:21.090
gone.
Tim Ferriss: You have to do it eventually,

01:56:21.090 --> 01:56:23.560
so do you want to let go of 20 feet at once?
Probably not.

01:56:23.560 --> 01:56:28.670
Jake Muise: Yeah. And they fall flat on their
ass. Or do you want to gradually let it go,

01:56:28.670 --> 01:56:33.060
so it's okay? And just the idea of seeing
it moving through your hands and knowing you

01:56:33.060 --> 01:56:39.010
don't want it to be this sudden jerk has made
it easier for me to make those small decisions

01:56:39.010 --> 01:56:47.159
knowing at 18, 19, hopefully, we'll see they're
gone, right? And they'll always be mine, or

01:56:47.159 --> 01:56:52.440
ours, but it's one of the few parenting advice
I've ever heard that made me, that created

01:56:52.440 --> 01:56:58.469
an actionable thing where I said, "Okay, I
need to make this slow and smooth and the

01:56:58.469 --> 01:57:01.780
right transition for them." So thank you,
Ric. It was a good one.

01:57:01.780 --> 01:57:07.590
Tim Ferriss: Amazing. I want to ask just as
we start coming to a close, a few questions.

01:57:07.590 --> 01:57:13.699
One is, what are your hopes and dreams for
the next few years? I want you to also answer

01:57:13.699 --> 01:57:17.290
something, which is one of the questions,
you may not remember this, but it was a very

01:57:17.290 --> 01:57:21.920
important question for me to ask very early
on, which was I asked something along the

01:57:21.920 --> 01:57:28.659
lines of how do you ensure that you do not
overharvest or end up doing more damage than

01:57:28.659 --> 01:57:33.889
good? Because people respond to incentives.
If this economic model works, if the product

01:57:33.889 --> 01:57:38.290
is very, very high quality, which it is, if
there is a lot of demand, how do you ensure

01:57:38.290 --> 01:57:44.010
that you're not seduced by the sirens of capitalism
and end up becoming something antithetical

01:57:44.010 --> 01:57:50.500
to your current ethos? So maybe that ties
in just in terms of rules, objectives that

01:57:50.500 --> 01:57:56.560
you've set for yourselves as a company, but
broadly speaking, also hopes and goals over

01:57:56.560 --> 01:57:59.310
the next few years.
Jake Muise: Okay. I'll answer the second first

01:57:59.310 --> 01:58:05.560
in that our mission is to achieve balance
and defining balance, we don't actually get

01:58:05.560 --> 01:58:11.090
to do that, which is probably really important.
We have the tools to make sure we can measure,

01:58:11.090 --> 01:58:15.520
like we talked about during surveys, we have
the tools to make sure we measure really accurately

01:58:15.520 --> 01:58:21.530
how many we're harvesting and the impact it's
having. But the individual landowners ultimately

01:58:21.530 --> 01:58:26.770
make that decision. And then the community
at large gets to weigh in as well through

01:58:26.770 --> 01:58:33.420
public lands.
If we've done our job really well, deer will

01:58:33.420 --> 01:58:39.610
find a place within our food systems, not
within our critical ecosystems that collect

01:58:39.610 --> 01:58:46.949
water and not at densities that impact our
reefs. They will find a way into our food

01:58:46.949 --> 01:58:50.760
systems in these mid-elevations. And the ranches
are already pointing to that. They get to

01:58:50.760 --> 01:58:54.349
decide what densities they want, and they
can only have densities that are healthy,

01:58:54.349 --> 01:58:59.330
i.e., they grow enough grass that those animals
are healthy and sustainable. So we get to

01:58:59.330 --> 01:59:04.540
be the tool to help find balance. We actually
don't get to answer the question, which is

01:59:04.540 --> 01:59:10.390
the guarding agent between us doing more harm
than good. They get to make that decision,

01:59:10.390 --> 01:59:13.199
which is awesome. The community gets to make
that decision.

01:59:13.199 --> 01:59:18.710
And there'll be some ranchers that decide
— ranchers are grass farmers, right? There'll

01:59:18.710 --> 01:59:23.610
be some ranchers that decide, "I'm not doing
cattle anymore, I want more deer in this area."

01:59:23.610 --> 01:59:28.880
But we have the tool to manage them the same
way they would a cattle herd. And that's really

01:59:28.880 --> 01:59:36.090
my hope for what we're doing at Maui Nui is,
we find the balance that's best for all of

01:59:36.090 --> 01:59:42.770
our communities: ecosystem communities, food
system communities, our human communities,

01:59:42.770 --> 01:59:47.920
nearshore fisheries. And the great part about
what we've talked about today is where there's

01:59:47.920 --> 01:59:53.000
just so much iteration to what we do, and
it's so variable that it's the infinite game

01:59:53.000 --> 01:59:57.340
for us. That we're just going to keep doing
it every single year trying to find balance.

01:59:57.340 --> 02:00:00.750
Because some years you'll have more rain,
some years you won't, and we'll constantly

02:00:00.750 --> 02:00:04.770
be able to go back to our community and say,
"How is this working? What does this look

02:00:04.770 --> 02:00:09.440
like?"
And I think maybe balance is maybe my hope

02:00:09.440 --> 02:00:17.119
for life as a whole and personal goals. I
don't have, this is not a multi-billion dollar

02:00:17.119 --> 02:00:22.760
business for us. I'm so content. We already
love the place that we're in. I don't need

02:00:22.760 --> 02:00:28.190
bigger, brighter, shinier things. If I've
done a great job letting go of that rope really

02:00:28.190 --> 02:00:34.360
slowly and collectively, like we've talked
about today, growing incredible people. I

02:00:34.360 --> 02:00:40.380
never realized how much value I'd find in
growing teams. I didn't know I was going to

02:00:40.380 --> 02:00:44.790
come back to that like I did with sports and
seeing the extraordinary group of people that

02:00:44.790 --> 02:00:53.739
are now at Maui Nui and being the shield that
ensures there's only ever great people there,

02:00:53.739 --> 02:00:59.440
ourselves included in that process, man, that
is hope enough and mission enough. If I can

02:00:59.440 --> 02:01:04.310
do those two things really well, that'll be
more than enough. I think that'll go a long

02:01:04.310 --> 02:01:07.040
way in serving community and hopefully my
kids and —

02:01:07.040 --> 02:01:11.010
Tim Ferriss: The whole shebang.
Jake Muise: Yeah.

02:01:11.010 --> 02:01:17.760
Tim Ferriss: Dig it. Last question is the
billboard question. This is sometimes a dead

02:01:17.760 --> 02:01:22.460
end, so I'll take the blame if it goes sideways,
but if it goes well, I'll give you all the

02:01:22.460 --> 02:01:29.630
credit. Simple question. There is a billboard,
metaphorically speaking. You can get a message

02:01:29.630 --> 02:01:36.961
out to hundreds of millions, billions of people.
Could be an image, could be a quote, could

02:01:36.961 --> 02:01:41.610
be a word, could be a question, could be anything.
Could also be something you want to remind

02:01:41.610 --> 02:01:45.730
yourself of. Could be any of those things.
What might you put on that billboard?

02:01:45.730 --> 02:01:50.429
Jake Muise: I remember thinking about this,
listening to your podcast for years, and it

02:01:50.429 --> 02:01:56.060
may not make perfect sense when you read it,
but the first business book I ever read was

02:01:56.060 --> 02:01:59.849
called The Little Red Book of Selling. It
was this tiny little thing that I got.

02:01:59.849 --> 02:02:02.950
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I know the book.
Jake Muise: And a quote in there stuck with

02:02:02.950 --> 02:02:09.750
me for years, and it was, "Pick up the phone."
And early in my career that meant pick up

02:02:09.750 --> 02:02:16.659
the phone, take any opportunity, do everything,
just always, always pick up the phone. And

02:02:16.659 --> 02:02:23.520
later, as I've grown, it's turned into less
of "Pick up the phone and take those opportunities,"

02:02:23.520 --> 02:02:28.270
but "Address the thing that is in front of
you that you don't want to. Have the uncomfortable

02:02:28.270 --> 02:02:34.580
conversation, pick up the phone, just do it."
And what it's resulted in is a very rapid

02:02:34.580 --> 02:02:41.710
iteration of taking action constantly and
not being a lot of downtime in between it.

02:02:41.710 --> 02:02:45.850
And now it means today just addressing all
of those hard things that are in front of

02:02:45.850 --> 02:02:48.730
growth or with my family.
And it's just been like, so if that billboard

02:02:48.730 --> 02:02:53.571
said, "Pick up the phone," that could mean
something to somebody really different. Maybe

02:02:53.571 --> 02:02:58.631
that's your mom that's calling that you don't
want to pick up the phone. But for me, it

02:02:58.631 --> 02:03:02.580
has constantly iterated and it's been this
crazy thing that has stuck with me for — I

02:03:02.580 --> 02:03:06.630
mean, I read that book 20 years ago and it's
just this thing that plays in my head all

02:03:06.630 --> 02:03:10.420
the time. When I see something that I don't
want to do or I know that has to get done

02:03:10.420 --> 02:03:14.909
immediately I say, "Pick up the phone." And
I just like, I'm able to just do it immediately.

02:03:14.909 --> 02:03:18.750
Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: "Pick up the phone." Good reminder.

02:03:18.750 --> 02:03:25.680
What a good reminder. Jake Muise. Français.
So nice to see you, man.

02:03:25.680 --> 02:03:26.873
Jake Muise: Always so good to see you, my
friend.

02:03:26.873 --> 02:03:34.170
Tim Ferriss: And people can check out Maui
